1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gnaeus Pinarius Cornelius Clemens
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pinarius Cornelius Clemens (fl. 1st century AD) was a Roman military officer and senator who was appointed Suffect consul during the reign of Vespasian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly originating from Hispania, Clemens Pinarius was admitted into the Senate sometime during the reign of the emperor Nero (54-68). Prior to AD 74, most probably either AD 71 or 72, he was appointed consul suffectus. Followin
2 CONC g this he was appointed curator exercitus qu sacrarum locorum publicorum (or the official responsible for maintaining the religious and public buildings of Rome).
2 CONT
2 CONT Clemens Pinarius’ next posting was as Legatus Augusti pro praetore (or imperial governor) of the province of Germania Superior, a position he held from around AD 72/73 to 74/75. Taking parts of the Legio VIII Augusta and the Legio
2 CONC  XI Claudia, he undertook a campaign on the right bank of the Rhine in Germania Magna, for which the emperor Vespasian awarded him the ornamenta triumphalia (or insignia of a triumph). Travelling through the sparsely populated Agr
2 CONC i Decumates in AD 74, Clemens Pinarius constructed a road from Argentoratum through to the upper Danube. This road shortened the distance between Augusta Vindelicorum and Mogontiacum by 160 km or seven days' march.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clemens Pinarius was buried in the town of Hispellum. Gnaeus Pinarius Cornelius Severus, the suffect consul of AD 112, was probably his grandson.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnaeus_Pinarius_Cornelius_Clemens
