1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Herodes Atticus bust, from his villa at Kephissia. mid-2nd century
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lucius Vibullius Hipparchus Tiberius Claudius Atticus Herodes''', otherwise known as '''Herodes Atticus (Ἡρῴδης ὁ Ἀττικός''', 101-177 was a very distinguished, rich Greek aristocrat who served as a Roman Senator and a sophist. 
2 CONC He is notable as a proponent in the Second Sophistic by Philostratus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry and Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus was a Greek of Athenian descent. His ancestry can be traceable to the Athenian noble woman Elpinice (a half sister of statesman Cimon and daughter of politician Miltiades the Younger). He claimed his lineage from t
2 CONC he Athenian King Theseus; the Athenian Monarch named Cecrops; King Aeacus and the God Zeus. He had an ancestor four generations removed from him called Polycharmus. Polycharmus from 9/8 BC-22/23, could have served as an Archon of 
2 CONC Athens. His family bore the Roman family name, Claudius. There is a possibility that a paternal ancestor of his, received Roman citizenship, from an unknown member of the Claudian gens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus was born to a distinguished and very rich family of consular rank He was born to a Roman Senator, Tiberius Claudius Atticus Herodes and the wealthy heiress Vibullia Alcia Agrippina. Herodes Atticus had a brother na
2 CONC med Tiberius Claudius Atticus Herodianus and a sister named Claudia Tisamenis. His maternal grandparents were Claudia Alcia and Lucius Vibullius Rufus, while his paternal grandfather was Hipparchus and his unnamed wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents were related as uncle and niece. His maternal grandmother with his father were sister and brother. His maternal uncle Lucius Vibullius Hipparchus was an Archon of Athens in the years 99-100 and his maternal cousin, Pub
2 CONC lius Aelius Vibullius Rufus was an Archon of Athens between 143-144.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus was born in Marathon, Greece. In his childhood years, he spent between Greece and Italy. According to Juvenal Satire III, he received an education in rhetoric and philosophy. Herodes Atticus was educated by many of
2 CONC  the best teachers from the Greek culture and Roman culture.[9] Throughout his life, Herodes Atticus remained entirely Greek in his cultural outlook.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roman Emperor Hadrian in 125 appointed him as Prefect of the free cities in the Roman province of Asia. He then later returned to Athens, where he became famous as a teacher. In the year 140, Herodes Atticus was elected and served
2 CONC  as an Archon of Athens. Later in 140, the Roman Emperor Antoninus Pius, invited him to Rome from Athens to educate his two adopted sons, who were future Roman Emperors Marcus Aurelius and Lucius Verus. Sometime after Herodes Atti
2 CONC cus came to Rome from Athens, he was betrothed to an aristocratic wealthy woman called Aspasia Annia Regilla, who was a relative to the wife of Antoninus Pius, Roman Empress Faustina the Elder. When Regilla and Herodes Atticus mar
2 CONC ried, Regilla was 14 years old and Herodes Atticus was 40 years old. As Herodes Atticus was in favor with the Emperor, as a mark of his friendship with the Emperor, Antoninus Pius appointed him as a consul in 143.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus and Regilla controlled a large area of land around the Third Mile of the Appian Way outside of Rome, which was known as the "Triopio" (from Triopas, King of Thessaly). For his remaining years he travelled between G
2 CONC reece and Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after his consulship, he returned to Greece with his wife and their children. Bust of Polydeukes - Altes Museum, Berlin
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus was the teacher of three notable students who were Achilles, Memnon and Polydeuces (Polydeukes). "The aged Herodes Atticus in a public paroxysm of despair at the death of his maybe eromenos, Polydeukes, commissione
2 CONC d games, inscriptions and sculptures on a lavish scale and then died, inconsolable, shortly afterwards."
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus had a distinguished reputation for his literary work. Most of his works are now lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus was a philanthropist and a patron of public works. He funded a number of building projects which were:
2 CONT
2 CONT     A Stadium - Athens
2 CONT
2 CONT     Odeon - Athens
2 CONT
2 CONT     A theater at Corinth
2 CONT
2 CONT     A stadium at Delphi
2 CONT
2 CONT     The baths at Thermopylae
2 CONT
2 CONT     An aqueduct at Canusium in Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT     An aqueduct at Alexandria Troas
2 CONT
2 CONT     A nymphaeum (a monumental fountain) with his wife at Olympia
2 CONT
2 CONT     Various benefactions to the peoples of Thessaly, Epirus Euboea, Boeotia and Peloponnesus
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout his life, Herodes Atticus had a stormy relationship with the citizens of Athens, and before he died, made a reconciliation with them.[9] When he died, the citizens of Athens gave Herodes Atticus an honorary burial. His 
2 CONC funeral had taken place in the Panathinaiko Stadium in Athens, in which he commissioned to build. Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Regilla bore Herodes Atticus six children, of whom three survived to adulthood. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Son, Claudius – born and died in 141
2 CONT
2 CONT     Daughter, Elpinice – born as Appia Annia Claudia Atilia Regilla Elpinice Agrippina Atria Polla, 142-165
2 CONT
2 CONT     Daughter, Athenais – born as Marcia Annia Claudia Alcia Athenais Gavidia Latiaria, 143-161
2 CONT
2 CONT     Son, Atticus Bradua – born as Tiberius Claudius Marcus Appius Atilius Bradua Regillus Atticus, born 145
2 CONT
2 CONT     Son, Regillus – born as Tiberius Claudius Herodes Lucius Vibullius Regillus, 150-155
2 CONT
2 CONT     Unnamed child who died with Regilla or died even perhaps three months later in 160
2 CONT
2 CONT After Regilla died in 160, Herodes Atticus never married again. When he died in 177, his son Atticus Bradua and his grandchildren survived him. Sometime after his wife’s death, he adopted his cousin’s first grandson Lucius Vibulli
2 CONC us Claudius Herodes as his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Herodes Atticus and Regilla from the 2nd century until present are considered great benefactors in Greece, in particular in Athens. The couple is commemorated in Herodou Attikou Street and Rēgíllēs Street and Square, in downtown A
2 CONC thens. In Rome, their names are also recorded on modern streets, in the Quarto Miglio suburb close to the area of the Triopio.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
