1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Calpurnius_Piso_(consul_15_BC)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus (consul 15 BC)=
2 CONT '''Lucius Calpurnius L. f. L. n. Piso Caesoninus''' (PW 99) (48 BC - 32 AD) was a prominent Roman senator of the early principate. He was the son of Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus and brother of Calpurnia Pisonis, wife of Juliu
2 CONC s Caesar. He became a confidante of the emperors Augustus and Tiberius. His tenure as a pontifex was sometimes used by contemporary Romans to differentiate him from his contemporary, Lucius Calpurnius Piso the Augur.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus =
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus (PW 99) (48 BC-AD 32) was a prominent Roman senator of the early Empire. He was the son of Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus, consul in 58 BC, and brother of Calpurnia, the third and last wife of 
2 CONC Julius Caesar. He was a confidant of the emperors Augustus and Tiberius. His tenure as pontifex led him sometimes to be called Lucius Calpurnius Piso Pontifex, to differentiate him from his contemporary, Lucius Calpurnius Piso the
2 CONC  Augur, consul in 1 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Piso was consul in 15 BC, and shortly thereafter engaged in Mediolanum as proconsul. Cassius Dio refers to him as governor of Pamphylia in the years 13 to 11 BC; his province probably included Galatia. In 11 BC, he was sent to Thr
2 CONC ace as legatus pro praetore in order to put down a revolt. For his successes there, the senate honoured him with the ornamenta triumphalia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Piso may have also been proconsul of Asia and legate of Syria, but this is disputed. He was praefectus urbi from AD 13 to 32, and a trusted adviser to both Augustus and Tiberius. He was a member of the pontifical college and of th
2 CONC e Arval Brethren. He died in 32, and was honoured with a state funeral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Piso's achievements and independence were highly regarded. Horace dedicated his Ars Poëtica to him (cf. Carmen 2.12), and several epigrams by Antipater of Thessalonica are dedicated to Piso.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Calpurnius_Piso_Caesoninus_(consul_15_BC)
