1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Gracchus
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: I84717
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: GAIUS SEMPRONIUS GRACCHUS
2 CONT
2 CONT Given Name: GAIUS SEMPRONIUS
2 CONT
2 CONT Surname: GRACCHUS
2 CONT
2 CONT Sex: M
2 CONT
2 CONT _UID: C6636014B6B91B4B8E73703CE0D9E422DEF3
2 CONT
2 CONT Change Date: 11 Aug 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gracchus, Gaius Sempronius (153-121 bc), Roman soldier and statesman, brother of Tiberius, whose murder he sought to avenge.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his brother's death, Gaius was serving with the Roman army in Spain. He returned to Rome a year or two later, but took no major part in public affairs. Upon attaining the quaestorship (a position in financial admini
2 CONC stration) in 126 bc, he accompanied the army under Lucius Aurelius Orestes (consul in 126 bc) to Sardinia, then in a state of rebellion. Although the Roman Senate, with uneasy recollections of his brother Tiberius, sought to keep 
2 CONC Gaius from Rome by extending his term as quaestor, he strongly felt the obligation of avenging his brother's murder and accordingly returned to the capital without authorization. He became a candidate for tribune of the people and
2 CONC  was elected to that office in 123 bc. He then devoted himself to the enforcement of his brother's agrarian laws, which had gradually been allowed to lapse, and carried out important new legislation in the interest of the common p
2 CONC eople as opposed to that of the Senate and the aristocracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT To develop the resources of Italy and at the same time provide employment for the poor, Gaius inaugurated a program of road repair and construction throughout the country. He secured passage of a law entitling every citizen in Rom
2 CONC e to purchase grain at half price, and he formulated measures designed to curb the power of the Senate and to increase that of wealthy commoners. Gaius was reelected tribune in 122 bc. The aristocratic party, unable to check his r
2 CONC eforms by open opposition, resorted to the stratagem of offering, through Marcus Livius Drusus (who was cotribune with Gaius), still greater benefits; however, they had no intention of carrying out their promises, and civil strife
2 CONC  was intensified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Opimius, an implacable foe of Gaius, was elected consul in 121 bc, while Gaius himself failed to obtain his third term of tribuneship. He was then deserted by the majority of his supporters and was forced to escape across t
2 CONC he Tiber River. The following day his body was discovered on the Janiculum, a hill located on the west bank of the river.
2 CONT
