2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Pomponia=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Pomponia (fl. 212 BC) was a Roman woman who lived in the 3rd century BC. She came from a Roman noble family who were of plebs status, and were prominent knights or equestrians. She was the daughter of the consul Manius Pomponius M
2 CONC atho, consul in 233 BC [1] (who appears to have died in 211 BC), and was married possibly around 237 BC to Publius Cornelius Scipio, second surviving son of the Roman censor Lucius Cornelius Scipio of a prominent patrician family.
2 CONC  Her husband later became a general and statesman during the Second Punic War and was killed in battle in Hispania in 211 BC. By her marriage, Pomponia was the mother of at least two sons, the famous Roman general Publius Corneliu
2 CONC s Scipio Africanus Major (236-184 BC/183 BC) and Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus (fl. 183 BC).
2 CONT
2 CONT Livy's brief mentions of Pomponia show her to be a devout religious woman, much preoccupied with her household duties.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source :''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomponia
