1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manius_Pomponius_Matho
2 CONT
2 CONT =Manius Pomponius Matho=
2 CONT '''Manius Pomponius M.f. M.n. Matho''' (fl. 236 BC; d 211 BC) was a Roman consul and general who was elected consul for the year 233 BC with Quintus Fabius Maximus Verrucosses, later called Cunctator. He was also the maternal gran
2 CONC dfather of the general and statesman '''Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus Major'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT '''Matho''' was the brother of the consul '''Marcus Pomponius Matho''', consul 231 BC who died in 204 BC. Either man, but probably the latter, was the father of the plebeian aedile '''Marcus Pomponius Matho''', aedile in 206 BC, w
2 CONC ho was ordered to investigate the complaints of the Locrians against his kinsman Scipio Africanus.[2] '''Matho''' is best known as the grandfather of the great Roman general '''Scipio Africanus'''. Unfortunately, no historians cov
2 CONC er Scipio's early life, and state whether Scipio Africanus ever served under his maternal grandfather before leaving for Hispania. His daughter '''Pomponia''' was the wife of '''Publius Cornelius Scipio''', consul in 218 BC (kille
2 CONC d in 211 BC). According to William Smith, relying on Cicero, the name Matho was pronounced without the "h" and was sometimes written as Mato.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Manius Pomponius Matho=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Manius Pomponius M.f. M.n. Matho (fl. 236 BC; d 211 BC) was a Roman consul and general who was elected consul for the year 233 BC with Quintus Fabius Maximus Verrucosses, later called Cunctator. He was also the maternal grandfathe
2 CONC r of the general and statesman Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus Major.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his consulship, Matho carried on war against the Sardinians, and was granted a triumph for his victory over them. However, this victory was incomplete, because the war was carried on by his brother Marcus, who was a consul 
2 CONC in 231 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 217 BC, he was apparently chosen magister equitum to the dictator, Lucius Veturius Philo, and was elected praetor for the following year, 216 BC. There seems no reason for believing that the Matho, praetor of this year, was a d
2 CONC ifferent person from the consul of 233 BC, as the Romans were now at war with Hannibal, and were therefore anxious to appoint to the great offices of the state generals who had had experience in war. The lot, however, did not give
2 CONC  any military command to Matho, but the jurisdictio inter ewes Romanos et peregrines. After news had been received of the fatal battle of Cannae, Matho and his colleague, the praetor urbanus, summoned the senate to the curia Hosti
2 CONC lia to deliberate on what steps were to be taken. (Liv. xxii. 33, 35, 55, xxiii. 20, 24.) At the expiration of his office, Matho received as propraetor the province of Cisalpine Gaul, in 215 BC; for Livy says (xxiv. 10), in the ne
2 CONC xt year, b. c. 214, that the province of Gaul was continued to him. Livy, however, not only makes no mention of Matho's appointment in 215 BC, but expressly states (xxiii. 25) that in that year no army was sent into Gaul on accoun
2 CONC t of the want of soldiers. We can only reconcile these statements by supposing that Matho was appointed to the province but did not obtain any troops that year. He died in 211 BC, at which time he was one of the pontifices. (Liv. 
2 CONC xxvi. 23.) He was succeeded in that office by Gaius Livius Salinator.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Matho was the brother of the consul Marcus Pomponius Matho, a consul in 231 BC who died in 204 BC. Either man, but probably the latter, was the father of the plebeian aedile Marcus Pomponius Matho, aedile in 206 BC, who was ordere
2 CONC d to investigate the complaints of the Locrians against his kinsman Scipio Africanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matho is best known as the grandfather of the great Roman general Scipio Africanus. His daughter Pomponia was the wife of Publius Cornelius Scipio, a consul in 218 BC (killed in 211 BC).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to William Smith, relying on Cicero, the name Matho was pronounced without the "h" and was sometimes written as Mato.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manius_Pomponius_Matho
