1 NOTE {geni:about_me} For details see: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Aemilius_Mamercus
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Aemilius Mamercus was a Roman statesman who served as consul three times: in 484, 478 and 473 BC.[2][3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 484 BC, as consul, Aemilius led the Roman forces in battle against the Volsci and Aequi. The Romans were successful, and the Roman cavalry slaughtered many in the rout which followed.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Livy says that during his first consulship, Aemilius (together with his colleague Caeso Fabius Vibulanus) worked with the senate to oppose increases to the powers of the tribunes.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 478 BC, Aemilius led a Roman army successfully against the Etruscans.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT Aemilia gens
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Robert Maxwell Ogilvie, Commentary on Livy, books 1–5, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1965, pp. 404, 405.
2 CONT  Diodorus Siculus, II.38, II.52, II.64
2 CONT  Dionysius of Halicarnassus, Roman Antiquities, IX.37
2 CONT  Livy, Ab urbe condita, 2.42
2 CONT  Titus Livius, Ab Urbe Condita, ii. 49.
