1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Manlia_gens<br/>
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tusculum
2 CONT
2 CONT Manlia gens
2 CONT
2 CONT The gens Manlia was one of the oldest and noblest patrician houses at Rome, from the earliest days of the Republic until imperial times. The first of the gens to obtain the consulship was Gnaeus Manlius Cincinnatus, consul in 480 
2 CONC BC, and for nearly five centuries its members frequently held the most important magistracies. Many of them were distinguished statesmen and generals, and a number of prominent individuals under the Empire claimed the illustrious 
2 CONC Manlii among their ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Manlii were said to hail from the ancient Latin city of Tusculum, a ruined Roman city in the Alban Hills and today is amongst the largest ruins of a Roman city in the region. Tusculum was most famous in Roman times for the man
2 CONC y great and luxurious patrician country villas sited close to the city, yet a comfortable distance from Rome
2 CONT
2 CONT The nomen Manlia may be a patronymic surname, based on the praenomen Manius, presumably the name of an ancestor of the gens. As with many patrician gentes, the Manlii seem to have acquired plebeian branches as well, and one of the
2 CONC  family was tribune of the plebs in the time of Cicero. The plebeian Manlii were probably descended from freedmen of the patricians, from members who had gone over to the plebeians, or from unrelated persons who acquired the nomen
2 CONC  after obtaining the franchise from one of the Manlii. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Manlii used the praenomina Publius, Gnaeus, Aulus, Lucius, and Marcus. The Manlii Torquati also favored the name Titus, using primarily that, Aulus, and Lucius.
2 CONT
2 CONT A well-known story relates that after Marcus Manlius Capitolinus was condemned for treason, the Roman Senate decreed that henceforth none of the gens should bear the praenomen Marcus. However, this legend may have originated as a 
2 CONC way to explain the scarcity of the name amongst the Manlii, as the name was rarely used in later generations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest cognomen found amongst the Manlii is Cincinnatus, better known from the Quinctia gens. This name originally referred to a person with fine, curly hair. The descendants of Gnaeus Manlius Cincinnatus bore the surname Vu
2 CONC lso, meaning "plucked", perhaps chosen for its contrast to Cincinnatus. Münzer, noting that the cognomen Cincinnatus is missing from the older historians, supposed that it might be a mistake, and that Vulso was the original surnam
2 CONC e of the Manlian gens. The Manlii Vulsones flourished for over three hundred years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Manlii Capitolini were descended from the Vulsones, and first appear in the second half of the fifth century BC. The surname Capitolinus probably indicates that the family lived on the Capitoline Hill, although the role of Mar
2 CONC cus Manlius in saving the Capitol from the Gauls during the sack of Rome in 390 BC is also credited with establishing the name in his family. The surname was relatively short-lived amongst the Manlii, being replaced by that of Tor
2 CONC quatus. This surname was first acquired by Titus Manlius Capitolinus Imperiosus, who defeated a giant Gaul during a battle in 361 BC, and took his torque as a trophy, placing it around his own neck.[8] The descendants of Torquatus
2 CONC  remained prominent until the final decades of the Republic, and adopted the torque as an emblem upon their coins. Imperiosus, a cognomen borne by Torquatus and his father, was bestowed on account of their imperious manner. The Ma
2 CONC nlii Torquati were firmly aligned with the aristocratic party toward the end of the Republic, siding first with Sulla, then with Pompeius and the Liberatores. In later times, Torquatus was borne by the Junii Silani, who were desce
2 CONC nded from the Manlii.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Manlii Acidini rose to prominence during the Second Punic War, but achieved only one consulship, in 179, before fading into relative obscurity. They still flourished in the time of Cicero, who praises their nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT From coins of the Manlii featuring the inscriptions SER and SERGIA, Münzer concluded that one stirps of this gens bore the cognomen Sergianus, indicating descent from the Sergia gens. However, this probably referred to the tribus 
2 CONC Sergia; a plebeian branch of the Manlii used the name of their tribe to distinguish themselves from the patrician Manlii, a practice also found among the Memmii.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of the Republic, several early Manlii appear without cognomina, such as Quintus and Gnaeus Manlius, tribunes of the plebs in 69 and 58 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Members:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Manlii Cincinnati et Vulsones'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Publius Manlius, father of the consul Cincinnatus.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius P. f. Cincinnatus, consul in 480 BC, fought against the Etruscans, and was slain in battle.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius Cn. f. P. n. Vulso, consul in 474 BC, marched against Veii, who agreed to a forty-year truce, for which Manlius received an ovation. A number of scholars identify him with the decemvir of 451, but this seems doubt
2 CONC ful on chronological grounds; he was probably the decemvir's father.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius Cn. f. P. n. Vulso, one of the ambassadors sent to research Greek laws in 454 BC, and subsequently one of the decemvirs elected in 451.<br/>
2 CONT * Marcus Manlius Cn. f. Vulso, consular tribune in 420 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Publius Manlius M. f. Cn. n. Vulso, consular tribune in 400 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius (Vulso), consular tribune in 379 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius A. f. P. n. Vulso Longus, consul in 256 and 250 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius L. f. A. n. Vulso, praetor peregrinus in 218 BC, was an unsuccessful candidate for the consulship in 216.<br/>
2 CONT * Publius Manlius L. f. A. n. Vulso, praetor in BC 210, received Sardinia as his province. He minted coins during his magistracy.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius Cn. f. L. n. Vulso, aedile curule in 197 BC, praetor of Sicily in 195, and consul in 189 BC. As proconsul of Asia in 188-187, he negotiated the Treaty of Apamea with Antiochus III.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius Cn. f. L. n. Vulso, praetor in 197 BC, received Sicilia as his province. He was also legate to his brother Gnaeus, the consul of 189, during his campaign in Asia. In 188, he demanded from Antiochus III his oath to
2 CONC  uphold the Treaty of Apamea.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius Cn. f. L. n. Vulso, one of the triumvirs appointed to establish a colony in the territory of Thurii and Frentinum from 194 to 192 BC. He was praetor suffectus in 189, and consul in 178. He was assigned the province
2 CONC  of Cisalpine Gaul, whence he attacked and conquered Istria.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius Vulso, ambassador in Bythinia 149 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Manlii Capitolini'''<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marcus Manlius P. f. Vulso Capitolinus, consul or consular tribune in 434 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius Vulso Capitolinus, consular tribune in 422 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius A. f. Cn. n. Vulso Capitolinus, consular tribune in 405, 402, and 397 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius A. f. Cn. n. Vulso Capitolinus, the father of Marcus, consul in 392 BC, and Aulus, four times consular tribune. He is only known from his sons' filiation.<br/>
2 CONT * Quintus Manlius A. f. Cn. n. Vulso Capitolinus, consular tribune in 396 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Marcus Manlius T. f. A. n. Capitolinus, consul in 392 BC, the deliverer of the Capitol from the Gauls. He was also interrex in 387.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius T. f. A. n. Capitolinus, consular tribune in 389, 385, 383 and 370 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Publius Manlius A. f. A. n. Capitolinus, consular tribune in 379 and 367 BC. As dictator in 368, he nominated the first plebeian magister equitum, Gaius Licinius Calvus.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius A. f. A. n. Capitolinus Imperiosus, dictator in 363 BC, and father of Titus Manlius Imperiosus Torquatus. He was prosecuted in 362 by the tribune Marcus Pomponius for having stayed in power for more than six month
2 CONC s, and for his cruelty toward some citizens and his own son, the future Torquatus, who nonetheless forced Pomponius to drop the charges against his father.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius L. f. A. n. Capitolinus Imperiosus, consul in 359 and 357 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Manlii Torquati'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Titus Manlius L. f. A. n. Imperiosus Torquatus, military tribune in 361, dictator in 353, 349, and 320; and consul in 347, 344, and 340 BC, was a celebrated general, and won the name Torquatus for defeating a Gaulish champion in
2 CONC  single combat, and taking his torque as a trophy. He is equally remembered for the severe discipline that he imposed upon his eldest son during his final consulship.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f. L. n. Torquatus, while serving as prefect in 340 BC, he was put to death by his father, the consul, after disobeying orders to engage an enemy champion in single combat, hoping to replicate his father's feat.
2 CONC <br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, consul in 299 BC, thrown from his horse and killed.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, legate of Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus in the great campaign of 295 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus Atticus, censor in 247 BC, consul in 244 and 241. He received a triumph during his second consulship for having thwarted a revolt of the Falisci.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, consul in 235 and 224, censor in 231, propraetor in Sardinia in 215, and dictator in 208 BC. He was awarded a triumph in 235 for his campaign in Sardinia. He was also a pontiff.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, tribunus militum in 208 BC, he was killed with the consul Marcus Claudius Marcellus in a Carthaginian ambush near Petelia.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius A. f. T. n. Torquatus, praetor in 170 BC, consul in 165, and ambassador to Egypt in 162. He also became pontiff in 170.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius A. f. T. n. Torquatus, praetor in 167 BC, and consul in 164.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f. A. n. Torquatus, praetor circa 136 BC. He was defeated by the revolted slaves of Eunus in Sicily.<br/>
2 CONT * Decimus Junius Silanus Manlianus, a natural son of Titus Manlius Torquatus, the consul of 165 BC. He was adopted into the Junii Silani. He was praetor in 142.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius T. f. A. n. Torquatus, son of Titus Manlius Torquatus, the consul of 165 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, quaestor between 113 and 112 BC. He minted coins during his magistracy.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, son of the praetor of circa 136 BC. He died at an old age since he was still alive in 54 to serve as witness on behalf of Gnaeus Plancius.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius L. f. T. n. Torquatus, proquaestor in the staff of Sulla in 82 BC, with whom he minted coins. He was then praetor in 68, perhaps proconsul of Asia in 67, consul in 65, and finally proconsul of Macedonia in 64 and 
2 CONC 63.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f. T. n. Torquatus, possibly a praetor circa 69 BC. He studied under Apollonius Molon in Rhodes and was promised to the consulship, but died before he could be eligible.<br/>
2 CONT * Manlia T. f. T. n., married her cousin Aulus Manlius Torquatus, the praetor of 70 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Publius Cornelius Lentulus Spinther, the natural son of the consul of 65 BC, he was adopted into the Manlii by Titus Manlius Torquatus after the death of his natural son. The adoption was just an artifice to make him eligible to
2 CONC  the college of augurs, as it already counted a member of the gens Cornelia (Faustus Cornelius Sulla), but none of the Manlii. Spinther did not even make the pretext of changing his name.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius A. f. T. n. Torquatus, praetor in 70, then propraetor of Africa in 69, and quaesitor in 52. He was also a legate of Pompeius in 67, tasked with the surveillance of the east of Spain and the Balearic Islands. He mar
2 CONC ried his cousin, Manlia.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius L. f. L. n. Torquatus, member of the Quindecemviri Sacris Faciundis by 69 BC, triumvir monetalis in 65, praetor in 49, and propraetor between 48 and 46. He was a partisan of Pompeius. After the Battle of Thapsus i
2 CONC n 46, he failed to escape to Spain, and committed suicide with Metellus Scipio on a ship.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius L. f. L. n. Torquatus, quaestor of Gaius Vibius Pansa in 43 BC. He supported Brutus and Cassius, but survived the proscriptions of the Triumvirs, and later became a friend of Horace. He was perhaps a pontiff.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius A. f. A. n. Torquatus.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius A. f. A. n. Torquatus, present in the house of Caesar, where the case against Deiotarus was heard in 45 BC.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Manlii Acidini'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius L. f. Acidinus, praetor urbanus in 210 BC and proconsul of Hispania in 206, where he succeeded Scipio Africanus. He stayed in command there until 200.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius L. f. L. n. Fulvianus, the natural son of Quintus Fulvius Flaccus, was adopted by Lucius Manlius Acidinus, the praetor of 210 BC. Fulvianus was praetor in Hispania Citerior in 188 BC, proconsul in Spain from 187 t
2 CONC o 185, an ambassador to the Gauls, and one of the triumvirs appointed to establish a colony at Aquileia in 183, and finally consul in 179, with his natural brother, Quintus Fulvius Flaccus.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius L. f. L. n. Acidinus, a military tribune in 171 BC, and quaestor in 168.<br/>
2 CONT * Manlius Acidinus M. f., military tribune in 171 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Manlius Acidinus, an acquaintance of the younger Cicero in 45 BC.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Others'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Publius Manlius, epulo in 196, praetor in Hither Spain in 195. He was possibly expelled from the Senate by Cato the Censor for having kissed his wife in front of his daughter. Reintegrated as praetor in 182, he was sent to Farth
2 CONC er Spain, where he stayed as promagistrate for two years. He died in 180 at his return from Spain.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius Q. f., triumvir monetalis between 118 and 107 BC, and legate of Gaius Marius in 107, during the war against Jugurtha. He was one of the envoys sent to obtain Jugurtha's surrender. From the inscriptions on his coins
2 CONC , Münzer supposes that he bore the cognomen Sergianus.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius Mancinus, triumvir monetalis between 111 and 110 BC and tribune of the plebs in 107.<br/>
2 CONT * Aulus Manlius A. f. Q. n., quaestor in 80 BC. He minted gold coins during his magistracy, which shows he was a supporter of Sulla.<br/>
2 CONT * Lucius Manlius, possibly praetor in 79 BC, and proconsul in Transalpine Gaul in 78. He was defeated in Spain by Lucius Hirtuleius, Sertorius' legate.<br/>
2 CONT * Quintus Manlius A. f. Q. n., triumvir capitalis circa 77 BC, and tribune of the plebs in 69.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius, praetor in 72, defeated by Sertorius.<br/>
2 CONT * Manlius Priscus, a legate of Pompey in 65 BC, during the war against Mithridates VI.<br/>
2 CONT * Manlius Lentinus, legate of Gaius Pomptinus in Gallia Narbonensis, won a decisive victory over the Gauls, and captured the city of Ventia in 61 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Gnaeus Manlius, tribune of the plebs in 58 BC, proposed a law granting the libertini the right to vote as members of any tribe. The law was blocked by the praetor Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus. Manlius is sometimes confused with G
2 CONC aius Manilius, who proposed a similar law in 66 BC.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius T. f., may have been a legate in Spain between 45 and 42 BC, as he is named on a coin of Brutobriga. From the inscription, Münzer supposes that his cognomen was Sergianus.[133]<br/>
2 CONT * Quintus Manlius Ancharius Tarquitius Saturninus, consul suffectus in AD 62, and proconsul of Africa in 72 and 73.<br/>
2 CONT * Titus Manlius Valens, consul in AD 96, died the same year.[136]<br/>
2 CONT * Manlia Scantilla, wife of Marcus Didius Julianus, and Roman empress in AD 193.<br/>
2 CONT *  Anicius Manlius Severinus Boëthius, an influential Christian philosopher of the fifth century.
