0 @N6000000002188289354@ NOTE Marcus Plautius Silvanus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Plautius Silvanus was an ordinary consul in 2 BC,[1] and proconsul of Asia in 4 AD. He also served in Pannonia in 9 AD[2], Dalmatia[3] and Illyricum.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Dio Cassius, Epitome of Roman History LV.0 
1 CONT ^ Dio LV.34 
1 CONT ^ Dio LVI.12 
1 CONT ^ Velleius Paterculus II.112 
0 @N6000000002188289102@ NOTE Cornelia Pompeia Magna
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cornelia Pompeia Magna also known as Pompeia Magna Minor (Minor Latin for the younger, born after 47 BC and before 35 BC-?) was the daughter and youngest child to Pompeia Magna and suffect consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna. Cornelia’s
1 CONC  maternal grandparents were triumvir Pompey and his third wife Mucia Tertia, while her paternal grandparents were politician an elder Lucius Cornelius Cinna and an unnamed Roman woman. Her full-blooded brother was Gnaeus Cornelius
1 CONC  Cinna Magnus; she also had two half-brothers and one half-sister from her mother’s first marriage to senator Faustus Cornelius Sulla. Her mother had died before 35 BC and was raised by her father. She along with her brother were 
1 CONC born and raised in Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Very little is known on the life of Cornelia. Before 16, Cornelia had married Lucius Scribonius Libo consul of 16 and like her came from a senatorial family. This nobleman was actually a distant relative to Cornelia. Libo was a de
1 CONC scendant of her mother’s first marriage; his paternal grandmother was Cornelia’s elder half-sister. The Roman Emperor Tiberius, who charged Libo in planning a revolt against the emperor, stabbed Libo to death in 16.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cornelia and Libo had one daughter and only child called Scribonia who married the consul Marcus Licinius Crassus Frugi and had children.
0 @N6000000002188289060@ NOTE Lucius Cornelius Cinna (suffect consul 32 BC)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Cinna (flourished 1st century BC) was the son of an elder Lucius Cornelius Cinna, who was a supporter of politician Gaius Marius and his mother is an unnamed Roman woman. His sister was Cornelia Cinna minor, was t
1 CONC he first wife of dictator Gaius Julius Caesar and was the maternal uncle of their daughter Julia Caesaris. Cinna was a member of the Cornelius (gens), who were of patrician status.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna in 78 BC, allied himself with Marcus Aemilius Lepidus in overthrowing the Roman constitution of dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla. After the defeat and death of Lepidus in Sardinia, Cinna accompanied Marcus Perperna to join ge
1 CONC neral Quintus Sertorius in Spain. Caesar used Cinna, against various Roman Senators in the Roman Senate and was able to recall Cinna from exile back to Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to his father’s association with Marius and Sulla’s constitutions reforms, he was unable to promote his career, until Caesar’s rise. When Caesar became dictator, Cinna promoted to the praetorship. Although Cinna, strongly disa
1 CONC pproved of Caesar’s way of governing, he wouldn’t associate himself with the senators who wanted Caesar killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Caesar was assassinated in March 44 BC, the senators were in such rage of Cinna, they accidentally murdered tribune of the plebs Helvius Cinna, thinking it was he. When the murder of the tribune took place, Cinna was walking
1 CONC  in Caesar’s funeral procession. Cinna during the political chaos didn’t promote his career, even not claiming a Roman province, for him to govern. Cicero praises him for this act. Cinna in 32 BC, served as a suffect consul.
1 CONT
1 CONT After 47 BC, Cinna married Pompeia Magna, the daughter of triumvir Pompey and from his third wife Mucia Tertia. Cinna married Pompeia has her second husband. Pompeia married Cinna as his first wife. Pompeia had become a widow, who
1 CONC  her husband Faustus Cornelius Sulla had died in battle. Cinna became a stepfather to Pompeia’s children from her first marriage. Pompeia bore Cinna two children who were: a son Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus and a daughter Corneli
1 CONC a Pompeia Magna. His wife died before 35 BC and beyond this no more is known on Cinna.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopaedia 2002 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0763.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0764.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/2806.html 
