0 @N6000000002188289111@ NOTE Mucia Tertia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mucia Tertia was a Roman matrona that lived in the 1st century BC. She was the daughter of Quintus Mucius Scaevola, the pontifex maximus, consul in 95 BC. Her mother was a Licinia that divorced her father to marry Quintus Caeciliu
1 CONC s Metellus Nepos, in a scandal mentioned by several sources. Her name, Mucia Tertia, suggests that she was a third daughter, according to the Roman naming convention for women. Mucia had also two younger brothers from her mother's
1 CONC  second marriage (see Caecilius Metellus family tree), and she was a cousin (soror) of Q. Metellus Celer, consul in 60 BC, and of Q. Metellus Nepos, consul in 57 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla, as dictator, needed to secure Pompey's loyalty and to do that, he arranged his marriage to Mucia around 79 BC. This marriage resulted in three children: Gnaeus Pompeius (Pompey the Younger), the girl Pompeia Magna (married 
1 CONC to Faustus Cornelius Sulla) and Sextus Pompeius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Between 76 and 61 BC, Pompey spent most of the time away from Rome, campaigning in Hispania against Sertorius, in the Mediterranean Sea against the pirates and in the East, and fighting king Mithridates VI of Pontus. On his final 
1 CONC return, in 61 BC, Pompey sent Mucia a letter of divorce. According to Cicero's personal correspondence, the motive was adultery (it is said that she was one of Julius Caesar's many affairs). Mucia next married M. Aemilius Scaurus,
1 CONC  a step­son of the dictator Sulla. In 39 BC, Mucia, at the earnest request of the Roman people, went to Sicily to mediate between her son Sextus Pompey and Augustus. She was living at the time of the battle of Actium, 31 BC. Augus
1 CONC tus treated her with great respect.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Asconius, Pro Scauro, p.19, Orelli-Baiter (ed.) (1845). 
1 CONT Cicero, Epistulae ad Familiares, v, 2. 
1 CONT Cicero, Epistulae ad Atticum, i. 12. 
1 CONT Dio Cassius, xxxvii. 49, xlviii, 16, li. 2, lvi. 38. 
1 CONT Appian, Bellum Civile v. 69, 72. 
1 CONT Suetonius, Life of Julius Caesar, 50. 
1 CONT Plutarch, Life of Pompey, 42. 
1 CONT Zonaras, x. 5. 
1 CONT Hieronymus, Adversus Jovinianum, i. 48. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the public domain Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology by William Smith (1867).
0 @N6000000002188289207@ NOTE Pompey
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus 
1 CONT
1 CONT Born September 29, 106 BC
1 CONT Rome 
1 CONT Died September 28, 48 BC
1 CONT Egypt
1 CONT
1 CONT Occupation Politician and military commander 
1 CONT Spouse Antistia
1 CONT Aemilia Scaura
1 CONT Mucia Tertia
1 CONT Julia
1 CONT Cornelia Metella 
1 CONT Pompey, Pompey the Great or Pompey the Triumvir [1] (Classical Latin abbreviation: CN·POMPEIVS·CN·F·SEX·N·MAGNVS[2], Gnaeus or Cnaeus Pompeius Magnus) (September 29, 106 BC–September 28, 48 BC), was a distinguished military and po
1 CONC litical leader of the late Roman Republic. Hailing from an Italian provincial background, after military triumphs he established a place for himself in the ranks of Roman nobility, and was given the cognomen of Magnus—the Great—by
1 CONC  Lucius Cornelius Sulla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey was a rival of Marcus Licinius Crassus and an ally to Gaius Julius Caesar. The three politicians dominated the Late Roman republic through a political alliance called the First Triumvirate. After the death of Crassus, Pompe
1 CONC y and Caesar became rivals, disputing the leadership of the Roman state in what is now called Caesar's civil war. Pompey fought on the side of the Optimates, the conservative faction in the Roman Senate, until he was defeated by C
1 CONC aesar. He then sought refuge in Egypt, where he was assassinated.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life and political debut 
1 CONT 2 Sicily and Africa 
1 CONT 3 Quintus Sertorius and Spartacus 
1 CONT 4 Rome's new frontier on the East 
1 CONT 4.1 Campaign against the Pirates 
1 CONT 4.2 Pompey in the East 
1 CONT 5 Pompey’s return to Rome 
1 CONT 6 Caesar and the First Triumvirate 
1 CONT 7 Confrontation to war 
1 CONT 8 Civil War and assassination 
1 CONT 9 Historic view 
1 CONT 10 Marriages and offspring 
1 CONT 11 Chronology of Pompey's life and career 
1 CONT 12 Pompey in literature and the arts 
1 CONT 13 Notes 
1 CONT 14 Further reading 
1 CONT 15 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life and political debut
1 CONT His father Pompeius Strabo was an extremely wealthy man from the Italian region of Picenum but his family was not a part of the ancient families who had dominated Roman politics. Nevertheless, his father had climbed through the tr
1 CONC aditional cursus honorum being quaestor in 104 BC, praetor in 92 BC, and consul in 89 BC. Pompey had scarcely left school before he was summoned to serve under his father in the Social war. He fought under him in 89 against the It
1 CONC alians, at the age of seventeen, fully involved in his father's military and political affairs, and he would continue with his father until Strabo's death two years afterward. According to Plutarch, who was sympathetic to Pompey, 
1 CONC he was very popular, and considered a look-alike of Alexander the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father died in 87 BC, in the conflicts between Gaius Marius and Lucius Cornelius Sulla, leaving young Pompey in control of his family affairs and fortune. For the next few years the Marian party had possession of Italy; and ac
1 CONC cordingly Pompey, who adhered to the aristocratic party, was obliged to keep in the background. Returning to Rome he was prosecuted for misappropriation of plunder but quickly acquitted. His acquittal was certainly helped by the f
1 CONC act that he was betrothed to the judge's daughter, Antistia. Pompey sided with Sulla after his return from Greece in 83 BC. Sulla was expecting trouble with Gnaeus Papirius Carbo's regime and found the 23-year-old Pompey and the t
1 CONC hree veteran legions very useful. When Pompey (displaying great military abilities in opposing the Marian generals by whom he was surrounded) succeeded in joining Sulla, he was saluted by the latter with the title of Imperator. Th
1 CONC is political alliance boosted Pompey's career greatly and Sulla, now the Dictator in absolute control of the Roman world, persuaded Pompey to divorce his wife and marry his stepdaughter Aemilia Scaura, who was pregnant by her curr
1 CONC ent husband, in order to bind his young ally more closely to him.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sicily and Africa
1 CONT Although his young age kept him a privatus (a man holding no political office of—or associated with—the cursus honorum), Pompey was a very rich man and a talented general in control of three veteran legions. Moreover, he was ambit
1 CONC ious for glory and power. During the remainder of the war in Italy Pompey distinguished himself as one of the most successful of Sulla's generals; and when the war in Italy was brought to a close, Sulla sent Pompey against the Mar
1 CONC ian party in Sicily and Africa. Happy to acknowledge his wife's son-in-law's wishes, and to clear his own situation as dictator, Sulla first sent Pompey to recover Sicily from the Marians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey easily made himself master of the island in 82 BC. Sicily was strategically very important, since the island held the majority of Rome's grain supply. Without it, the city population would starve and riots would certainly e
1 CONC nsue. Pompey dealt with the resistance with a harsh hand, executing Gnaeus Papirius Carbo and his supporters. When the citizens complained about his methods he replied with one of his most famous quotations: "Won't you stop citing
1 CONC  laws to us who have our swords by our sides?" Pompey routed the opposing forces in Sicily and then in 81 BC he crossed over to the Roman province of Africa, where he defeated Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and the Numidian king Hiar
1 CONC bas, after a hard-fought battle.
1 CONT
1 CONT After this continued string of unbroken victories, Pompey was proclaimed Imperator by his troops on the field in Africa. He earned the nickname adulescentulus carnifex ("teenage butcher" or "The Butcher Boy") at this time due to h
1 CONC is savagery in dealing with the remnant Marians. On his return to Rome in the same year, he was received with enthusiasm by the people, and was greeted by Sulla with the cognomen Magnus, (meaning "the Great"), with most commentato
1 CONC rs suspecting that Sulla gave it as a cruel and ironic joke; it was some time before Pompey made widespread use of it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey, however, not satisfied with this distinction, demanded a triumph for his African victories, which Sulla at first refused; Pompey himself refused to disband his legions and appeared with his demand at the gates of Rome wher
1 CONC e, amazingly, Sulla gave in, overcome by Pompey's importunity, and allowing him to have his own way. However, in an act calculated to cut Pompey down to size, Sulla had his own triumph first, then allowed Metellus Pius to triumph,
1 CONC  relegating Pompey to a third triumph in quick succession, on the assumption that Rome would become bored by the third one. Accordingly, Pompey attempted to enter Rome in triumph towed by an elephant. As it happened, it would not 
1 CONC fit through the gate and some hasty re-planning was needed, much to the embarrassment of Pompey and amusement of those present.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Quintus Sertorius and Spartacus
1 CONT
1 CONT Bust of Pompey in the Residenz, Munich.Pompey's reputation for military genius, and occasional bad judgment, continued when, after suppressing the revolt by Lepidus (whom he had initially supported for consul, against Sulla's wish
1 CONC es), he demanded proconsular imperium (although he had not yet served as Consul) to go to Hispania (the Iberian Peninsula, comprising modern Spain and Portugal) to fight against Quintus Sertorius, a Marian general. The aristocracy
1 CONC , however, now beginning to fear the young and successful general, was reluctant to provide him with the needed authority. Pompey countered by refusing to disband his legions until his request was granted. However in Hispania Sert
1 CONC orius had for the last three years successfully opposed Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius, one of the ablest of Sulla's generals, and ultimately it became necessary to send the latter some effectual assistance. As a result, the Sena
1 CONC te, with considerable lack of enthusiasm, determined to send Pompey to Hispania against Sertorius, with the title of proconsul, and with equal powers to Metellus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey remained in Hispania between five and six years 76–71 BC; but neither he nor Metellus was able to achieve a clean victory or gain any decisive advantage on the battlefield over Sertorius. But when Sertorius was treacherousl
1 CONC y murdered by his own officer Marcus Perperna Vento in 72, the war was speedily brought to a close. Perperna was easily defeated by Pompey in their first battle, and the whole of Hispania was subdued by the early part of the follo
1 CONC wing year 71.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the months after Sertorius' death, however, Pompey revealed one of his most significant talents; a genius for the organization and administration of a conquered province. Fair and generous terms extended his patronage throughou
1 CONC t Hispania and into southern Gaul. While Crassus was facing Spartacus late in the Third Servile War in 71 BC, Pompey returned to Italy with his army. In his march toward Rome he came upon the remains of the army of Spartacus and c
1 CONC aptured five thousand Spartacani who had survived Crassus and were attempting to flee. Pompey cut these fugitives to pieces, and therefore claimed for himself, in addition to all his other exploits, the glory of finishing the revo
1 CONC lt. His attempt to take credit for ending the Servile war was an act that infuriated Crassus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Disgruntled opponents, especially Crassus, said he was developing a talent for showing up late in a campaign and taking all the glory for its successful conclusion. This growing enmity between Crassus and Pompey would not be resol
1 CONC ved for over a decade. Back in Rome, Pompey was now a candidate for the consulship; and although he was ineligible by law, inasmuch as he was absent from Rome, had not yet reached the legal age, and had not held any of the lower o
1 CONC ffices of the state, still his election was certain. His military glory had charmed people, admirers saw in Pompey the most brilliant general of the age; and as it was known that the aristocracy looked upon Pompey with jealousy, m
1 CONC any people ceased to regard him as belonging to this party, and hoped to obtain, through him, a restoration of the rights and privileges of which they had been deprived by Sulla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey on December 31, 71 BC, entered the city of Rome in his triumphal car, a simple eques, celebrating his second extralegal triumph for the victories in Hispania. In 71 BC, at only 35 years of age (see cursus honorum), Pompey w
1 CONC as elected Consul for the first time, serving in 70 BC as partner of Crassus, with the overwhelming support of the Roman population.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Rome's new frontier on the East
1 CONT In his consulship (70 BC), Pompey openly broke with the aristocracy, and became the great popular hero. By 69 BC, Pompey was the darling of the Roman masses, although many Optimates were deeply suspicious of his intentions. He pro
1 CONC posed and carried a law, restoring to the tribunes the power of which they had been deprived by Sulla. He also afforded his powerful aid to the Lex Aurelia, proposed by the praetor Lucius Aurelius Cotta, by which the judices were 
1 CONC to be taken in future from the senatus, equites, and tribuni aerarii, instead of from the senators exclusively, as Sulla had ordained. In carrying both these measures Pompey was strongly supported by Caesar, with whom he was thus 
1 CONC brought into close connection. For the next two years (69 and 68 BC) Pompey remained in Rome. His primacy in the State was enhanced by two extraordinary proconsular commands, unprecedented in Roman history.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Campaign against the Pirates
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey on a coin by his son Sextus Pompeius.In 67 BC, two years after his consulship, Pompey was nominated commander of a special naval task force to campaign against the pirates that controlled the Mediterranean. This command, li
1 CONC ke everything else in Pompey's life, was surrounded with polemic. The conservative faction of the Senate was most suspicious of his intentions and afraid of his power. The Optimates tried every means possible to avoid it. Signific
1 CONC antly, Caesar was again one of a handful of senators who supported Pompey's command from the start. The nomination was then proposed by the Tribune of the Plebs Aulus Gabinius who proposed the Lex Gabinia, giving Pompey command in
1 CONC  the war against the Mediterranean pirates, with extensive powers that gave him absolute control over the sea and the coasts for 50 miles inland, setting him above every military leader in the East. This bill was opposed by the ar
1 CONC istocracy with the utmost vehemence, but was carried.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pirates were at this time masters of the Mediterranean, and had not only plundered many cities on the coasts of Greece and Asia, but had even made descents upon Italy itself. As soon as Pompey received the command, he began to
1 CONC  make his preparations for the war, and completed them by the end of the winter. His plans were crowned with complete success. Pompey divided the Mediterranean into thirteen separate areas, each under the command of one of his leg
1 CONC ates. In forty days he cleared the Western Sea of pirates, and restored communication between Hispania, Africa, and Italy. He then followed the main body of the pirates to their strongholds on the coast of Cilicia; and after defea
1 CONC ting their fleet, he induced a great part of them, by promises of pardon, to surrender to him. Many of these he settled at Soli, which was henceforward called Pompeiopolis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ultimately it took Pompey all of a summer to clear the Mediterranean of the danger of pirates. In three short months (67-66 BC), Pompey's forces had swept the Mediterranean clean of pirates, showing extraordinary precision, discip
1 CONC line, and organizational ability; so that, to adopt the panegyric of Cicero:[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT "Pompey made his preparations for the war at the end of the winter, entered upon it at the commencement of spring, and finished it in the middle of the summer." 
1 CONT The quickness of the campaign showed that he was as talented a general at sea as on land, with strong logistic abilities. Pompey was the hero of the hour.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Pompey in the East
1 CONT Pompey was employed during the remainder of this year and the beginning of the following in visiting the cities of Cilicia and Pamphylia, and providing for the government of the newly-conquered districts. During his absence from R
1 CONC ome (66 BC), Pompey was nominated to succeed Lucius Licinius Lucullus in the command, take charge of the Third Mithridatic War and fight Mithridates VI of Pontus in the East. Lucullus, a well-born patrician, made it known that he 
1 CONC was incensed at the prospect of being replaced by a "new man" such as Pompey. Pompey responded by calling Lucullus a "Xerxes in a toga." Lucullus shot back by calling Pompey a "vulture" because he was always fed off the work of ot
1 CONC hers, referring to his new command in the present war, as well as Pompey's actions at the climax of the war against Spartacus. The bill conferring upon him this command was proposed by the tribune Gaius Manilius, and was supported
1 CONC  by Cicero in an oration which has come down to us (pro Lege Manilia). Like the Gabinian law, it was opposed by the whole weight of the aristocracy, but was carried triumphantly. The power of Mithridates had been broken by previou
1 CONC s victories of Luculus, and it was only left to Pompey to bring the war to a conclusion. This command essentially entrusted Pompey with the conquest and reorganization of the entire Eastern Mediterranean. Also, this was the second
1 CONC  command that Caesar supported in favor of Pompey.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey in the Temple of Jerusalem, by Jean FouquetOn the approach of Pompey, Mithridates retreated towards Armenia, but he was defeated; and as Tigranes the Great now refused to receive him into his dominions, Mithridates resolved
1 CONC  to plunge into the heart of Colchis, and thence make his way to his own dominions in the Cimmerian Bosporus. Pompey now turned his arms against Tigranes; but the Armenian king submitted to him without a contest, and was allowed t
1 CONC o conclude a peace with the republic. In 65 BC Pompey set out in pursuit of Mithridates, but he met with much opposition from the Iberians and Albanians; and after advancing as far as the River Phasis (now Fax or Rioni River), he 
1 CONC resolved to leave these districts. He accordingly retraced his steps, and spent the winter at Pontus, which he made into a Roman province. In 64 BC he marched into Syria, deposed the king Antiochus XIII Asiaticus, and made that co
1 CONC untry also a Roman province. In 63 BC, he advanced further south, in order to establish the Roman supremacy in Phoenicia, Coele-Syria, and Israel. After that he captured Jerusalem. At the time Judaea was racked by civil war betwee
1 CONC n two Jewish brothers who created religious factions: Hyrcanus and Aristobulus. The civil war was causing instability, and it exposed Pompey's unprotected flank. He felt that he had to act. Both sides gave money to Pompey for assi
1 CONC stance, and a picked delegation of Pharisees went in support of Hyrcanus. Pompey decided to link forces with the good-natured Hyrcanus, and their joint army of Romans and Jews besieged Jerusalem for three months, after which it wa
1 CONC s taken from Aristobulus. Aristobulus was crafty, though, and later succeeded in temporarily usurping the throne from Hyrcanus. Subsequently, King Herod I executed Hyrcanus in 31 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey entered the Holy of Holies; this was only the second time that someone had dared to penetrate into this sacred spot. He went to the Temple to satisfy his curiosity about stories he had heard about the worship of the Jewish 
1 CONC people. He made it a priority to find out whether or not the Jews had no physical statue or image of God in their most sacred place of worship. To Pompey, it was inconceivable to worship a God without portraying him in a type of p
1 CONC hysical likeness, like a statue. What Pompey saw was unlike anything he had seen on his travels. He found no physical statue, religious image, or pictorial description of the Hebrew God. Instead, he saw the Torah scrolls, and was 
1 CONC thoroughly confused.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was during the war in Judea that Pompey heard of the death of Mithridates.
1 CONT
1 CONT With Tigranes as a friend and ally of Rome, the chain of Roman protectorates now extended as far east as the Black Sea and the Caucasus. The amount of tribute and bounty Pompey brought back to Rome was almost incalculable: Plutarc
1 CONC h lists 20,000 talents in gold and silver added to the treasury, and the increase in taxes to the public treasury rose from 50 million to 85 million drachmas annually. His administrative brilliance was such that his dispositions e
1 CONC ndured largely unchanged until the fall of Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey conducted the campaigns of 65 to 62 BC and Rome annexed much of Asia firmly under its control. He imposed an overall settlement on the kings of the new eastern provinces, which took intelligent account of the geographical a
1 CONC nd political factors involved in creating Rome's new frontier on the East.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Pompey’s return to Rome
1 CONT His third Triumph took place on the 29 September 61 BC, on Pompey's 45th birthday, celebrating the victories over the pirates and in the Middle East, and was to be an unforgettable event in Rome. Two entire days were scheduled for
1 CONC  the enormous parade of spoils, prisoners, army and banners depicting battle scenes to complete the route between Campus Martius and the temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus. To conclude the festivities, Pompey offered an immense tri
1 CONC umphal banquet and made several donations to the people of Rome, enhancing his popularity even further.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although now at his zenith, by this time Pompey had been largely absent from Rome for over 5 years and a new star had arisen. Pompey had been busy in Asia during the consternation of the Catiline Conspiracy, when Caesar pitted his
1 CONC  will against that of the Consul Cicero and the rest of the Optimates. His old colleague and enemy, Crassus, had loaned Caesar money. Cicero was in eclipse, now hounded by the ill-will of Publius Clodius and his factional gangs. N
1 CONC ew combinations had been made and the conquering hero had been out of touch.
1 CONT
1 CONT Back in Rome, Pompey deftly dismissed his armies, disarming worries that he intended to spring from his conquests into domination of Rome as Dictator. Pompey sought new allies and pulled strings behind the political scenes. The Op
1 CONC timates had fought back to control much of the real workings of the Senate; in spite of his efforts, Pompey found their inner councils were closed to him. His settlements in the East were not promptly confirmed. The public lands h
1 CONC e had promised his veterans were not forthcoming. From now on, Pompey's political maneuverings suggest that, although he toed a cautious line to avoid offending the conservatives, he was increasingly puzzled by Optimate reluctance
1 CONC  to acknowledge his solid achievements. Pompey's frustration led him into strange political alliances.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Caesar and the First Triumvirate
1 CONT Although Pompey and Crassus distrusted each other, by 61 BC their grievances pushed them both into an alliance with Caesar. Crassus' tax farming clients were being rebuffed at the same time Pompey's veterans were being ignored. Th
1 CONC us entered Caesar, 6 years younger than Pompey, returning from service in Hispania, and ready to seek the consulship for 59 BC. Caesar somehow managed to forge a political alliance with both Pompey and Crassus (the so-called First
1 CONC  Triumvirate). Pompey and Crassus would make him Consul, and he would use his power as Consul to force their claims. Plutarch quotes Cato the Younger as later saying that the tragedy of Pompey was not that he was Caesar's defeated
1 CONC  enemy, but that he had been, for too long, Caesar's friend and supporter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar's tempestuous consulship in 59 brought Pompey not only the land and political settlements he craved, but a new wife: Caesar's own young daughter, Julia. Pompey was supposedly besotted with his bride. After Caesar secured hi
1 CONC s proconsular command in Gaul at the end of his consular year, Pompey was given the governorship of Hispania Ulterior, yet was permitted to remain in Rome overseeing the critical Roman grain supply as curator annonae, exercising h
1 CONC is command through subordinates. Pompey efficiently handled the grain issue, but his success at political intrigue was less sure.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Optimates had never forgiven him for abandoning Cicero when Publius Clodius forced his exile. Only when Clodius began attacking Pompey was he persuaded to work with others towards Cicero's recall in 57 BC. Once Cicero was back
1 CONC , his usual vocal magic helped soothe Pompey's position somewhat, but many still viewed Pompey as a traitor for his alliance with Caesar. Other agitators tried to persuade Pompey that Crassus was plotting to have him assassinated.
1 CONC  Rumor (quoted by Plutarch) also suggested that the aging conqueror was losing interest in politics in favor of domestic life with his young wife. He was occupied by the details of construction of the mammoth complex later known a
1 CONC s Pompey's Theater on the Campus Martius; not only the first permanent theater ever built in Rome, but an eye-popping complex of lavish porticoes, shops, and multi-service buildings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar, meanwhile, was gaining a greater name as a general of genius in his own right. By 56 BC, the bonds between the three men were fraying. Caesar called first Crassus, then Pompey, to a secret meeting in the northern Italian t
1 CONC own of Lucca to rethink both strategy and tactics. By this time, Caesar was no longer the amenable silent partner of the trio. At Lucca it was agreed that Pompey and Crassus would again stand for the consulship in 55 BC. At their 
1 CONC election, Caesar's command in Gaul would be extended for an additional five years, while Crassus would receive the governorship of Syria, (from which he longed to conquer Parthia and extend his own achievements). Pompey would cont
1 CONC inue to govern Hispania in absentia after their consular year. This time, however, opposition to the three men was electric, and it took bribery and corruption on an unprecedented scale to secure the election of Pompey and Crassus
1 CONC  in 55 BC. Their supporters received most of the important remaining offices. The violence between Clodius and other factions were building and civil unrest was becoming endemic.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Confrontation to war
1 CONT The triumvirate was about to end, its bonds snapped by death: first, Pompey's wife (and at that time Caesar's only child), Julia, died in 54 BC in childbirth; later that year, Crassus and his army were annihilated by the Parthian 
1 CONC armies at the Battle of Carrhae. Caesar's name, not Pompey's, was now firmly before the public as Rome's great new general. The public turmoil in Rome resulted in whispers as early as 54 that Pompey should be made dictator to forc
1 CONC e a return to law and order. After Julia's death, Caesar sought a second matrimonial alliance with Pompey, offering a marital alliance with his grandniece Octavia (future emperor Augustus's sister). This time, Pompey refused. In 5
1 CONC 2 BC, he married Cornelia Metella, daughter of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Scipio, one of Caesar’s greatest enemies, and continued to drift toward the Optimates. It can be presumed that the Optimates had deemed Pompey the lesser of
1 CONC  two evils.
1 CONT
1 CONT In that year, the murder of Publius Clodius and the burning of the Curia (the Senate House) by an inflamed mob led the Senate to beg Pompey to restore order, which he did with ruthless efficiency. The trial of the accused murderer
1 CONC , Titus Annius Milo, is notable in that Cicero, counsel for the defense, was so shaken by a Forum seething with armed soldiers that he was unable to complete his defense. After order was restored, the suspicious Senate and Cato, s
1 CONC eeking desperately to avoid giving Pompey dictatorial powers, came up with the alternative of entitling him sole Consul without a colleague; thus his powers, although sweeping, were not unlimited.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Caesar was fighting against Vercingetorix in Gaul, Pompey proceeded with a legislative agenda for Rome, which revealed that he was now covertly allied with Caesar's enemies. While instituting legal and military reorganizatio
1 CONC n and reform, Pompey also passed a law making it possible to be retroactively prosecuted for electoral bribery—an action correctly interpreted by Caesar's allies as opening Caesar to prosecution once his imperium was ended. Pompey
1 CONC  also prohibited Caesar from standing for the consulship in absentia, although this had frequently been allowed in the past, and in fact had been specifically permitted in a previous law. This was an obvious blow at Caesar's plans
1 CONC  after his term in Gaul expired. Finally, in 51 BC, Pompey made it clear that Caesar would not be permitted to stand for Consul unless he turned over control of his armies. This would, of course, leave Caesar defenseless before hi
1 CONC s enemies. As Cicero sadly noted, Pompey had begun to fear Caesar. Pompey had been diminished by age, uncertainty, and the harassment of being the chosen tool of a quarreling Optimate oligarchy. The coming conflict was inevitable.
1 CONC [4]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Civil War and assassination
1 CONT Main article: Caesar's civil war
1 CONT
1 CONT The Flight of Pompey after Pharsalus, by Jean FouquetIn the beginning, Pompey claimed he could defeat Caesar and raise armies merely by stamping his foot on the soil of Italy, but by the spring of 49 BC, with Caesar crossing the R
1 CONC ubicon and his invading legions sweeping down the peninsula, Pompey ordered the abandonment of Rome. His legions retreated south towards Brundisium, where Pompey intended to find renewed strength by waging war against Caesar in th
1 CONC e East. In the process, almost unbelievably, probably thinking that Caesar would not dare, neither Pompey nor the Senate thought of taking the vast treasury with them, which was left conveniently in the Temple of Saturn when Caesa
1 CONC r and his forces entered Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Escaping Caesar by a hair in Brundisium, Pompey regained his confidence during the siege of Dyrrhachium, in which Caesar lost 1000 men. Yet, by failing to pursue at the critical moment of Caesar's defeat, Pompey threw away the cha
1 CONC nce to destroy Caesar's much smaller army. As Caesar himself said, "Today the enemy would have won, if they had had a commander who was a winner" (Plutarch, 65). According to Suetonius, it was at this point that Caesar said that "
1 CONC that man (Pompey) does not know how to win a war." With Caesar on their backs, the conservatives led by Pompey fled to Greece. Caesar and Pompey had their final showdown at the Battle of Pharsalus in 48 BC. The fighting was bitter
1 CONC  for both sides but eventually was a decisive victory for Caesar. Like all the other conservatives, Pompey had to run for his life. He met his wife Cornelia and his son Sextus Pompeius on the island of Mytilene. He then wondered w
1 CONC here to go next. The decision of running to one of the eastern kingdoms was overruled in favor of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his arrival in Egypt, Pompey's fate was decided by the counselors of the young king Ptolemy XIII. While Pompey waited offshore for word, they argued the cost of offering him refuge with Caesar already en route for Egypt. It 
1 CONC was decided to murder Caesar's enemy to ingratiate themselves with him. On September 29, his 58th birthday, the great Pompey was lured toward a supposed audience on shore in a small boat in which he recognized two old comrades-in-
1 CONC arms, Achillas and Lucius Septimius. They were to be his assassins. While he sat in the boat, studying his speech for the king, they stabbed him in the back with sword and dagger. After decapitation, the body was left, contemptuou
1 CONC sly unattended and naked, on the shore. His freedman, Philipus, organized a simple funeral pyre and cremated the body on a pyre of broken ship's timbers.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Theodatus, the rhetorician, shows Caesar the head of Pompey; etching, 1820Caesar arrived a short time afterwards. As a welcoming present he received Pompey's head and ring in a basket. However, he was not pleased in seeing his riv
1 CONC al, once his ally and son-in-law, murdered by traitors. When a slave offered him Pompey's head, "he turned away from him with loathing, as from an assassin; and when he received Pompey's signet ring on which was engraved a lion ho
1 CONC lding a sword in his paws, he burst into tears" (Plutarch, Life of Pompey 80). He deposed Ptolemy XIII, executed his regent Pothinus, and elevated Ptolemy's sister Cleopatra VII to the throne of Egypt. Caesar gave Pompey's ashes a
1 CONC nd ring to Cornelia, who took them back to her estates in Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Historic view
1 CONT To the historians of his own and later Roman periods, the life of Pompey was simply too good to be true. No more satisfying historical model existed than the great man who, achieving extraordinary triumphs through his own efforts,
1 CONC  yet fell from power and influence and, in the end, was murdered through treachery.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a hero of the Republic, who seemed once to hold the Roman world in his palm only to be brought low by his own weak judgment and Caesar. Pompey was idealized as a tragic hero almost immediately after Pharsalus and his murder
1 CONC : Plutarch portrayed him as a Roman Alexander the Great, pure of heart and mind, destroyed by the cynical ambitions of those around him.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and offspring
1 CONT First wife, Antistia 
1 CONT Second wife, Aemilia Scaura (Sulla's stepdaughter) 
1 CONT Third wife, Mucia Tertia (whom he divorced for adultery, according to Cicero's letters) 
1 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius, executed in 45 BC, after the Battle of Munda 
1 CONT Pompeia Magna, married to Faustus Cornelius Sulla 
1 CONT Sextus Pompeius, who would rebel in Sicily against Augustus 
1 CONT Fourth wife, Julia (daughter of Caesar) 
1 CONT Fifth wife, Cornelia Metella (daughter of Metellus Scipio) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Chronology of Pompey's life and career
1 CONT 106 BC September 29 — born in Picenum 
1 CONT 83 BC — aligns with Sulla, after his return from the Mithridatic War against king Mithridates IV of Pontus; marriage to Aemilia Scaura 
1 CONT 82–81 BC — defeats Gaius Marius's allies in Sicily and Africa 
1 CONT 76–71 BC — campaign in Hispania against Sertorius 
1 CONT 71 BC — returns to Italy and participates in the suppression of a slave rebellion lead by Spartacus; second triumph 
1 CONT 70 BC — first consulship (with M. Licinius Crassus) 
1 CONT 67 BC — defeats the pirates and goes to Asia province 
1 CONT 66–61 BC — defeats king Mithridates of Pontus; end of the Third Mithridatic War 
1 CONT 64–63 BC — Pompey's March through Syria, the Levant, and Palestine 
1 CONT 61 BC September 29 — third triumph 
1 CONT 59 BC April — the first triumvirate is constituted; Pompey allies to Julius Caesar and Licinius Crassus; marriage to Julia (daughter of Julius Caesar) 
1 CONT 58–55 BC — governs Hispania Ulterior by proxy, construction of Pompey's Theater 
1 CONT 55 BC — second consulship (with M. Licinius Crassus) 
1 CONT 54 BC — Julia, dies; the first triumvirate ends 
1 CONT 52 BC — third consulship with Metellus Scipio; marriage to Cornelia Metella 
1 CONT 51 BC — forbids Caesar (in Gaul) to stand for consulship in absentia 
1 CONT 49 BC — Caesar crosses the Rubicon River and invades Italy; Pompey retreats to Greece with the conservatives 
1 CONT 48 BC — led by Pompey, the conservatives lose the battle of Pharsalus; Pompey runs away to Egypt, where he is killed on September 29 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Pompey in literature and the arts
1 CONT The historical character of Pompey plays a prominent role in several books from the Masters of Rome series of historical novels by Australian author Colleen McCullough.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey's rivalry with Julius Caesar supports the plot in George Bernard Shaw's Caesar and Cleopatra (play).
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey's entry into Jerusalem and the desecration of the Temple is depicted in the opening scene of Nicholas Ray's biblical epic King of Kings. Pompey is played by Conrado San Martín.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey is one of the key antagonists in the fourth season of Xena: Warrior Princess, portrayed by Australian actor Jeremy Callaghan. In the series, Pompey is beheaded by Xena in battle who then gives the head to Brutus to return t
1 CONC o Julius Caesar, telling Brutus to claim Pompey's death for himself without mentioning her role.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth Cranham as Gnaeus Pompey Magnus in RomeA fictionalized Gnaeus Pompey Magnus also plays a key role in the first season of the HBO/BBC television series Rome, where he is played by Kenneth Cranham.
1 CONT
1 CONT An opera seria composed during the baroque era, Handel's Giulio Cesare, is based on Cesare's reaction to Pompey's assassination (since the opera begins after the murder has occurred, Pompey never actually appears as a character--o
1 CONC nly his severed head when presented to the horrified Cesare). Typically, works composed in the genre of opera seria were intended to present lessons of morality while depicting aristocracy in a flattering light. In the case of Han
1 CONC del's Giulio Cesare, the Roman emperor prevails in the administration of justice against the evil Tolomeo (Ptolemy).
1 CONT
1 CONT
