0 @N6000000002188289137@ NOTE Publius Mucius Scaevola
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Publius Mucius Scaevola (c. 115 BC) was a prominent Roman politician and jurist. He was tribune in 141 BC, praetor in 136 BC, and consul in 133 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his consulship, Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio opposed Tiberius Gracchus running for a second consecutive tribuneship. Mucius said that he would allow Gracchus to run since that seemed to be the wishes of the peopl
1 CONC e and he did not want to resort to violence to prevent him from running. Scipio though continued to protest and led the mob that killed Gracchus. Mucius did not condemn Scipio for these actions despite his earlier comments.
1 CONT
1 CONT He later became Pontifex Maximus in 131 BC after the death of his brother, Publius Licinius Crassus Dives Mucianus. He is the last Pontifex Maximus to have published the Annales Maximi, the list of events of the year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gaius Fulvius Flaccus and Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus Africanus Consul of the Roman Republic
1 CONT with Lucius Calpurnius Piso Frugi
1 CONT 133 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Publius Popillius Laenas and Publius Rupilius 
0 @N6000000002187712722@ NOTE Atia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Atia Balba is the name of the three daughters of Julia (the second eldest sister of Julius Caesar) and Marcus Atius Balbus, one of whom was the mother of Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Atia Balba Prima 
1 CONT 2 Atia Balba Caesonia 
1 CONT 2.1 Popular culture 
1 CONT 3 Atia Balba Tertia 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Atia Balba Prima
1 CONT Atia Balba Prima was the mother of Quintus Pedius, the suffect consul of 43 BC. Her grandson, also named Quintus Pedius, was a deaf painter beloved by Augustus (a portrait of the deaf painter, who died in 13, can be seen at [1].
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Atia Balba Caesonia
1 CONT Atia Balba Caesonia (85 BC-43 BC) married the Macedonian governor and senator Gaius Octavius. Their children were Octavia Thurina Minor and Gaius Octavius Thurinus, later Augustus. In 59 BC, Octavius died on his way to Rome to sta
1 CONC nd for the consulship, and Atia married Lucius Marcius Philippus, the consul of 56 BC and a supporter of Julius Caesar. He loved raising his stepchildren alongside his own son and daughter from a previous marriage and arranged Oct
1 CONC avia's first marriage, to the consul and senator Gaius Claudius Marcellus Minor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Atia was a religious and caring matron. Tacitus considered her as an ideal Roman matron. Suetonius' account of Augustus, mentions the divine omens she experienced before and after his birth:
1 CONT
1 CONT When Atia had come in the middle of the night to the solemn service of Apollo, she had her litter set down in the temple and fell asleep, while the rest of the matrons also slept. On a sudden a serpent glided up to her and shortly
1 CONC  went away. When she awoke, she purified herself, as if after the embraces of her husband, and at once there appeared on her body a mark in colours like a serpent, and she could never get rid of it; so that presently she ceased ev
1 CONC er to go to the public baths. In the tenth month after that Augustus was born and was therefore regarded as the son of Apollo. Atia too, before she gave him birth, dreamed that her vitals were borne up to the stars and spread over
1 CONC  the whole extent of land and sea, while Octavius dreamed that the sun rose from Atia's womb. 
1 CONT The day he was born the conspiracy of Catiline was before the House, and Octavius came late because of his wife's confinement; then Publius Nigidius, as everyone knows, learning the reason for his tardiness and being informed also
1 CONC  of the hour of the birth, declared that the ruler of the world had been born. 
1 CONT Clause 94.
1 CONT
1 CONT She had doubts about her son's legitimacy as Caesar's heir, and attempted to dissuade him from accepting his inheritance. She died during her son's first consulship, in August/September 43 BC. Octavian gave her the highest honours
1 CONC  at her funeral. Philippus later married one of her sisters.
1 CONT
1 CONT A portrait of Atia Balba Caesonia can be seen under Section 4. Parents at [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Popular culture
1 CONT A highly fictionalized version of Atia is a major character in the HBO/BBC 2 television series Rome. Rather than a pious and loving individual, she is portrayed as a licentious, self-absorbed, and manipulative schemer whose sexual
1 CONC  escapades included Mark Antony.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Atia Balba Tertia
1 CONT Atia Balba Tertia was the mother of Lucius Pinarius.
