0 @N6000000002187600079@ NOTE Marcus Fulvius Flaccus was a Roman consul in 125 B.C. and a supporter of the Gracchi brothers. Flaccus supported the agrarian commission of Tiberius Gracchus and suggested extending citizenship to Italian landowners. He was forced
1 CONC  to abandon his reforms when the Senate sent him to Gaul to help Massilia against an attack by the Saluvii.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Fulvius Flaccus was consul, but then in 122 he stood for the lower office of tribune. This made Flaccus the junior tribune under Gaius Gracchus. Flaccus was probably the only consul to become tribune after his year as consu
1 CONC l. 
1 CONT
1 CONT http://ancienthistory.about.com/library/bl/bl_flaccus.htm
0 @N6000000002187718137@ NOTE Cato the Younger
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Marcus Porcius Cato Uticencis (95 BC–46 BC), known as Cato the Younger to distinguish him from his great-grandfather Cato the Elder, was a politician and statesman in the late Roman Republic, and a follower of the Stoic philosophy
1 CONC . He is remembered for his legendary stubbornness and tenacity (especially in his lengthy conflict with Julius Caesar), as well as his immunity to bribes and famous distaste for corruption.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Political beginnings 
1 CONT 3 Cato and the Optimates 
1 CONT 4 Cato against the triumvirate 
1 CONT 5 Cato in Cyprus 
1 CONT 6 Cato in the Civil War 
1 CONT 7 After Cato 
1 CONT 8 Cato's descendants and marriages 
1 CONT 9 Chronology 
1 CONT 10 Fictional portrayals 
1 CONT 11 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT Cato was born in 95 BC in Rome, the son of Marcus Porcius Cato by his wife Livia Drusa. He lost both of his parents very early and moved to live in the house of his maternal uncle Marcus Livius Drusus, who also looked after Quintu
1 CONC s Servilius Caepio and Servilia Caepionis from Livia's first marriage, as well as Porcia Catones (Cato's sister), and Drusus Nero (Livius' adopted son). Drusus was assassinated when Cato was four years old.
1 CONT
1 CONT The legend of Cato's stubbornness began in his early years. Sarpedon, his tutor, reports a very obedient and questioning child, although slow in being persuaded of things and sometimes difficult. A story told by Plutarch tells of 
1 CONC Popaedius Silo, leader of the Marsii and involved in a highly controversial business in the Roman forum, made a visit to his friend Marcus Livius he met the children of the house. In a playful mood he asked the children's support 
1 CONC for his cause. All of them nodded and smiled except Cato, who stared at the guest with most suspicious looks. Silo demanded an answer from him and seeing no response took Cato and hanged him by the feet out of the window. Even the
1 CONC n, Cato would not say anything. Being a friend of the family, Lucius Cornelius Sulla, the Roman dictator, liked to talk with Cato and his inseparable effeminate half-brother Caepio, and appreciated his company even when the teenag
1 CONC er defied his opinions in public.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Political beginnings
1 CONT After receiving his inheritance, Cato moved from his uncle's house and began to study Stoic philosophy and politics. He began to live in a very modest way, as his great-grandfather Cato the Elder had famously done. Cato subjected 
1 CONC himself to violent exercise, and learned to endure cold and rain with a minimum of clothes. He ate only what was necessary and drank the cheapest wine on the market. This was entirely for philosophical reasons, since his inheritan
1 CONC ce would have permitted him to live comfortably. He remained in private life for a long time, rarely seen in public. But when he did appear in the forum, his speeches and rhetorical skills were most admired.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Cato was promised Aemilia Lepida, a patrician woman, she married Cornelius Scipio instead. He threatened to sue them both in the courts, but his friends convinced him to step aside and marry a woman called Atilia. By her,
1 CONC  he had a son, Marcus Porcius Cato, and a daughter, Porcia Catones, who would become the second wife of Marcus Junius Brutus.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a military tribune, Cato was sent to Macedon in 67 BC at the age of 28 and given command of a legion. He led his men from the front, sharing their work, food and sleeping quarters. He was strict in discipline and punishment but
1 CONC  ended being loved by his legionaries. While Cato was in service in Macedon, he received the news that his beloved half-brother was dying in Thrace. He immediately set off to see him and got there in time to watch Caepio die. Cato
1 CONC  was overwhelmed by grief and, for once in his life, he spared no expense to organize a lavish funeral for his brother. Caepio left his fortune to be divided between his daughter Servilia and Cato.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the end of his military commission in Macedon, Cato went on a private journey through the Roman provinces of the Middle East.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Cato and the Optimates
1 CONT On his return to Rome in 65 BC, Cato was elected to the position of quaestor. Like everything else in his life, he took great care to study the background necessary for the post, especially the laws relating to taxes. One of his f
1 CONC irst moves was to prosecute former quaestors for illegal appropriation of funds and dishonesty. Cato also prosecuted Sulla's informers, who had acted as head-hunters during his tyranny. They were accused first of illegal appropria
1 CONC tion of treasury money, and then of homicide. At the end of the year, Cato stepped down from his quaestorship but never ceased to keep an eye on the Treasury, always looking for irregularities.
1 CONT
1 CONT As senator, Cato was scrupulous and determined. He never missed a session of the senate and publicly criticized the ones who did so. From day one he aligned himself with the Optimates, the conservative faction of the senate. He ha
1 CONC d, though, some strange habits, excused by his supporters as extravagances, such as appearing sometimes in public with no shoes, no underwear or both; or drinking unwatered wine, which, to the Roman mind, was a very distasteful an
1 CONC d inelegant thing to do.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Propaganda cup of Cato, for his election campaign for Tribune of the Plebs of 62 BC (left cup). These cups, filled with food or drinks, were distributed in the streets to the people, and bore an inscription supporting the candidat
1 CONC e to the election.In 63 BC, he was elected tribune of the plebs and assisted Marcus Tullius Cicero, the junior consul of the year, in his office, especially in dealing with the Catiline conspiracy. Sergius Catilina, a noble patric
1 CONC ian, was leading a rebellion inside Rome, with the purpose of becoming king. Cicero and Cato annihilated the danger and prosecuted all the men involved and sentenced them to death (a very unusual thing for a Roman citizen). In the
1 CONC  public discussion on the subject, Julius Caesar advocated a sentence of life exile for the conspirators.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cato's political, and personal, differences with Caesar date from this day. In a meeting of the senate dedicated to the Catilina affair, Cato harshly reproached Caesar for reading personal messages while the senate was in session 
1 CONC . Moreover, he accused him of conspiracy and suggested that he was working on Catilina's behalf. Caesar replied that it was only a love letter. Not believing the poor excuse, Cato took the paper from his hands and read it. Unfortu
1 CONC nately for him, Caesar was right: it was indeed a love letter from his mistress Servilia Caepionis, Cato's half-sister. This quickly turned into a huge scandal. Servilia was divorced from her husband and the Roman senators started
1 CONC  to look out for their households, since Caesar was notorious for liking to sleep with his political enemies' wives. Cato soon discovered he was one of his victims and immediately divorced his wife Atilia.
1 CONT
1 CONT He then married Marcia Philippa, the daughter of Lucius Marcius Philippus. A few years later, however, his friend Quintus Hortensius, an old man known for his rhetorical skills, asked for the hand of Cato's daughter from his previ
1 CONC ous marriage. But at the time, Porcia Catones was married to Calpurnius Bibulus, who was unwilling to let her go. Instead Cato took the surprising step of divorcing his Marcia and giving her to Quintus Hortensius. After Hortensius
1 CONC ' death, Cato married Marcia for the second time, taking possession of part of Hortensius' inheritance.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Cato against the triumvirate
1 CONT After the Catilina conspiracy and the scandal about his wife, Cato turned all his political skills to sabotaging everything that Caesar and his triumvirate allies (Pompey and Crassus) wanted to do. In 61 BC, Pompey returned from h
1 CONC is Mithridatic campaign with two ambitions: to celebrate a triumph, and become consul for the second time. In order to achieve both goals, he asked the senate to postpone consular elections until after his triumph. At first, due t
1 CONC o Pompey's enormous popularity, the senate was willing to oblige him. Then Cato intervened and made such a scandal about the issue that the senate forced Pompey to choose. The result was Pompey's third triumph, one of the most mag
1 CONC nificent ever seen in Rome. The next year he played the same trick on Caesar, returning from Hispania Ulterior, but Caesar chose to lose the right to the triumph and run for the consulship (which he got).
1 CONT
1 CONT When Caesar became consul, Cato opposed every law he suggested, especially the agrarian laws that established farmlands for the poor Roman citizens and Pompey's veterans. On one occasion, Caesar lost his patience and ordered Cato 
1 CONC arrested when he was making another speech against him at the rostra. So many Senators protested this by threatening to go to prison with Cato that Caesar relented. Upon his subsequent release he did not moderate his opposition to
1 CONC  the triumvirs, unsuccessfully attempting to prevent Caesar's five-year appointment as governor of Illyria and Cisalpine Gaul.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Cato in Cyprus
1 CONT Cato made such a nuisance of himself that Caesar and his triumvirate allies resolved that the best course was to get him out of town. The incentive was too good to refuse: the governorship of the new province of Cyprus. Cato accus
1 CONC ed them of trying to get rid of him, but eventually accepted the honour of being governor above being praetor.
1 CONT
1 CONT This task would show the world how much a Stoic philosopher Cato was. The province was rich both in gold and opportunities for extortion. Against common practice, he took none and prepared immaculate accounts for the senate. Accor
1 CONC ding to Plutarch, Cato raised the enormous sum of 7,000 talents of silver for the Roman treasury. He thought about every unexpected event, even to tying ropes to the coffers with a big piece of cork on the other end, so they could
1 CONC  be located in the event of a shipwreck. Unfortunately, luck played him a trick. Of his perfect accounting books, none survived: the one he had was burnt, the other were lost at sea with the freedman carrying it. Only Cato's untai
1 CONC nted reputation saved him from extortion charges.
1 CONT
1 CONT The senate of Rome recognized the effort made in Cyprus and offered him a reception in the city, an extraordinary praetorship, and other privileges, all of which he stubbornly refused as an unlawful honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Cato in the Civil War
1 CONT The triumvirate of Caesar, Pompey and Crassus was broken in 54 BC at the same time as Cato's election as praetor. Feeling his enemy in trouble, Cato and the conservative faction of the senate spent the coming years trying to force
1 CONC  the recall of Caesar from Gaul. It was a time of political turmoil, when demagogues like Publius Clodius Pulcher tried to make their political careers by wooing the crowds and resorting to violence. Cato fought them all and ended
1 CONC  as Pompey's ally and political advisor.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 49 BC, Caesar crossed the Rubicon accompanied by his thirteenth legion to fight for his right to run for a second consulship. The conservators, now led by Pompey, abandoned the city and ran to Greece, with Cato, an utter enemy 
1 CONC of Caesar, among them. The Optimates army was defeated by Caesar in the battle of Pharsalus (48 BC) and Pompey himself was killed shortly afterwards when he fled to Egypt. Cato and Metellus Scipio did not yet concede defeat and es
1 CONC caped to the province of Africa to continue resistance from Utica. Due to his presence in this city, Cato is sometimes referred to as Cato Uticensis (from Utica). Caesar went after them and, in February 46 BC, defeated their army 
1 CONC at the Battle of Thapsus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Being in Utica, Cato did not participate in the battle and, unwilling to live in a world led by Caesar, and refusing even implicitly to grant Caesar the power to pardon him, he committed suicide. Legend has it that Cato attempted 
1 CONC to commit suicide by falling on his own sword. His servants found him on the ground and stitched up and bandaged his wound. Cato waited till they left him and then tore off the bandages and the stitches with his fingers and pulled
1 CONC  out his own intestines, thereby completing his suicide attempt.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT After Cato
1 CONT Cato is remembered as a Stoic philosopher and one of the most active paladins of the decaying Republic. His high moral standards and incorruptible virtue gained him praise even from his political enemies. After Cato's death, Cicer
1 CONC o wrote a manifest eulogizing these qualities, to which Caesar (who never forgave him for all the obstructions) answered with his Anticato speech.
1 CONT
1 CONT Republicans under the empire remembered him fondly, and the poet Lucan, writing under Nero, made Cato the hero of Book IX of his unfinished epic, the Pharsalia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Dante's The Divine Comedy, Cato is portrayed as the guardian of the seaward approach to the island of purgatory. In Canto I, Dante writes of Cato:
1 CONT
1 CONT I saw close by me a solitary old man, worthy, by 
1 CONT his appearance, of so much reverence that never 
1 CONT son owed father more. 
1 CONT Long was his beard and mixed with white hair, 
1 CONT similar to the hairs of his head, which fell to his 
1 CONT breast in two strands. 
1 CONT The rays of the four holy lights so adorned his 
1 CONT face with brightness that I saw him as if the sun 
1 CONT had been before him. 
1 CONT Cato's life is immortalized in Joseph Addison's play, Cato, A Tragedy, which George Washington often quoted.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Cato's descendants and marriages
1 CONT First wife, Atilia (divorced for adultery) 
1 CONT Porcia Catones, married first to Calpurnius Bibulus, then to Brutus 
1 CONT Marcus Porcius Cato, later killed in the Second Battle of Philippi 
1 CONT Second (and third) wife, Marcia Phillipa 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Chronology
1 CONT 95 BC – Birth in Rome 
1 CONT 67 BC – Military tribune in Macedon 
1 CONT 65 BC – Quaestor in Rome 
1 CONT 63 BC – Tribune of the Plebs, Catilina's conspiracy 
1 CONT 60 BC – Forces Caesar to choose between consulship and triumph 
1 CONT 59 BC – Opposes Caesar's laws 
1 CONT 58 BC – Governorship of Cyprus 
1 CONT 54 BC – Praetor 
1 CONT 49 BC – Caesar crosses the Rubicon and invades Italy; Cato goes with Pompey to Greece 
1 CONT 48 BC – Battle of Pharsalus, Pompey defeated; Cato goes to Africa 
1 CONT 46 BC – 
1 CONT Defeated in the Battle of Thapsus 
1 CONT Commits suicide 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Fictional portrayals
1 CONT In the television series Rome, the character Cato, loosely based on the historical Cato, is played by actor Karl Johnson.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 2002 miniseries Julius Caesar, Cato is played by Christopher Walken.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Hughes-Hallett, Lucy. Heroes: A History of Hero Worship. Alfred A. Knopf, New York, New York, 2004. ISBN 1-4000-4399-9. 
1 CONT Plutarch. Cato the Younger.
0 @N6000000002187718257@ NOTE Lucius Appuleius Saturninus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Lucius Appuleius Saturninus, Roman demagogue and tribune.
1 CONT
1 CONT As quaestor (104 BC) he superintended the importation of corn at Ostia, but had been removed by the Senate (an unusual proceeding), and replaced by M. Aemilius Scaurus, one of the chief members of the government party. He does not
1 CONC  appear to have been charged with incapacity or mismanagement, and the injustice of his dismissal drove him into the arms of the popular party.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 103 he was elected tribune. He entered into an agreement with G. Marius, and in order to gain the favour of his soldiers proposed that each of his veterans should receive an allotment of 100 jugera of land in Africa. He was als
1 CONC o chiefly instrumental in securing the election of Marius to his fourth consulship (102 BC). An opportunity of retaliating on the nobility was afforded him by the arrival (101 BC) of ambassadors from Mithradates VI of Pontus, with
1 CONC  large sums of money for bribing the senate; compromising revelations were made by Saturninus, who insulted the ambassadors. He was brought to trial for violating the law of nations, and only escaped conviction by an ad misericord
1 CONC iam appeal to the people. To the first tribunate of Saturninus is probably to be assigned his law on majestas, the exact provisions of which are unknown, but its object was probably to strengthen the power of the tribunes and the 
1 CONC popular party; it dealt with the minuta majestas (diminished authority) of the Roman people, that is, with all acts tending to impair the integrity of the Commonwealth, being thus more comprehensive than the modern word "treason."
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the chief objects of Saturninus 's hatred was Quintus Caecilius Metellus Numidicus, who, when censor, endeavoured to remove Saturninus from the senate on the ground of immorality, but his colleague refused to assent. In ord
1 CONC er to ingratiate himself with the people, who still cherished the memory of the Gracchi, Saturninus took about with him Equitius, a paid freedman, who gave himself out to be the son of Tiberius Gracchus. Although the mother of the
1 CONC  Gracchi refused to acknowledge him, the people stoned Metellus because he would not admit his claim to citizenship. Equitius was afterwards elected tribune. Marius, on his return to Rome after his victory over the Cimbri, finding
1 CONC  himself isolated in the senate, entered into a compact with Saturninus and his ally Gaius Servilius Glaucia, and the three formed a kind of triumvirate, supported by the veterans of Marius and the needy rabble. By the aid of brib
1 CONC ery and assassination Marius was elected (100 BC) consul for the sixth time, Glaucia praetor, and Saturninus tribune for the second time. Saturninus now brought forward an agrarian law, an extension of the African law already allu
1 CONC ded to. It was proposed that all the land north of the Padus (Po) lately in possession of the Cimbri, including that of the independent Celtic tribes which had been. temporarily occupied by them, should be held available for distr
1 CONC ibution among the veterans of Marius. This was unjust, since the land was really the property of the provincials who had been dispossessed by the Cimbri.
1 CONT
1 CONT Colonies were to be founded in Sicily, Achaea and Macedonia, on the purchase of which the "Tolosan gold," the temple treasures embezzled by Q. Servilius Caepio (praetor 110), was to be employed. Further, Italians were to be admitt
1 CONC ed to these colonies, and as they were to be burgess colonies, the right of the Italians to equality with the Romans was thereby partially recognized. This part of the bill was resented by many citizens, who were unwilling to allo
1 CONC w others, to share their privileges. A clause provided that, within five days after the passing of the law, every senator should take an oath to observe it, under penalty of being expelled from the senate and heavily fined. All th
1 CONC e senators subsequently took the oath except Metellus, who went into exile. Saturninus also brought in a bill, the object of which was to gain the support of the rabble by supplying corn at a nominal price. The quaestor Q. Servili
1 CONC us Caepio declared that the treasury could not stand the strain, and Saturninus's own colleagues interposed their veto. Saturninus ordered the voting to continue, and Caepio dispersed the meeting by violence. The senate declared t
1 CONC he proceedings null and void, because thunder had been heard; Saturninus replied that the senate had better remain quiet; otherwise the thunder might be followed by hail. The bills (leges Appuleiae) were finally passed by the aid 
1 CONC of the Marian veterans.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marius, finding himself overshadowed by his colleagues and compromised by their excesses, thought seriously of breaking with them, and Saturninus and Glaucia saw that their only hope of safety lay in their retention of office. Sat
1 CONC urninus was elected tribune for the third time for the year beginning December 10, 100, and Glaucia, although at the time praetor and therefore not eligible until after the lapse of two years, was a candidate for the consulship. M
1 CONC arcus Antonius Orator was elected without opposition; the other government candidate, Gaius Memmius, who seemed to have the better chance of success, was beaten to death by the hired agents of Saturninus and Glaucia, while the vot
1 CONC ing was actually going on. This produced a complete revulsion of public feeling. The senate met on the following day, declared Saturninus and Glaucia public enemies, and called upon Marius to defend the State. Marius had no altern
1 CONC ative but to obey. Saturninus, defeated in a pitched battle in the Forum (December 10), took refuge with his followers in the Capitol, where, the water supply having been cut off, they were forced to capitulate. Marius, having ass
1 CONC ured them that their lives would be spared, removed them to the Curia Hostilia, intending to proceed against them according to law. But the more impetuous members of the aristocratic party climbed onto the roof, stripped off the t
1 CONC iles, and stoned Saturninus and many others to death. Glaucia, who had escaped into a house, was dragged out and killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Appian, Bell. civ. i. 28-33; Diod. Sic. xxxvi 12; Plutarch, Marius, 28-30; Livy, Epit. 69; Florus iii. 16; Vell. Pat. ii. 12; Auctor ad Herennium i. 21; Aurelius Victor, De viris illustribus, 73; Orosius v. 17; Cicero, Pro Balbo, 
1 CONC 21, 48, Brutus, 62, De oratore, ii. 49, De haruspicum responsis, 19, Pro Sestio, Pro Rabirio, passim; Mommsen, Hist. of Rome (Eng. trans.), bk. iv. ch. 6; G Long, Decline of the Roman Republic, ii. ch. 10; E. Klebs in Pauly-Wissow
1 CONC a's Realencyclopädie, ii. 1 (1896).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain.
0 @N6000000002188289232@ NOTE Gaius Lucilius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gaius Lucilius (c. 180 BC - 103 BC), the earliest Roman satirist, of whose writings only fragments remain, was born at Suessa Aurunca in Campania.
1 CONT
1 CONT The dates assigned by Jerome for his birth and death are 148 BC and 103 BC or 102 BC. But it is impossible to reconcile the first of these dates with other facts recorded of him, and the date given by Jerome must be due to an erro
1 CONC r, the true date being about 180 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT We learn from Velleius Paterculus that he served under Scipio Aemilianus at the siege of Numantia in 134. We learn from Horace that he lived on the most intimate terms of friendship with Scipio and Laelius, and that he celebrated 
1 CONC the exploits and virtues of the former in his satires.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fragments of those books of his satires which seem to have been first given to the world (books xxvi.-xxix.) clearly indicate that they were written in the lifetime of Scipio. Some of these bring the poet before us as either corre
1 CONC sponding with, or engaged in controversial conversation with, his great friend. One line "Percrepa pugnam Popilli, facta Corneli cane" in which the defeat of Marcus Popillius Laenas, in 138 BC, is contrasted with the subsequent su
1 CONC ccess of Scipio, bears the stamp of having been written while the news of the capture of Numantia was still fresh.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is in the highest degree improbable that Lucilius served in the army at the age of fourteen; it is still more unlikely that he could have been admitted into the familiar intimacy of Scipio and Laelius at that age. It also seems
1 CONC  an impossibility that between the ages of fifteen and nineteen--i.e. between 133 BC and 129 BC, the year of Scipio's death--he could have come before the world as the author of an entirely new kind of composition, and one which, 
1 CONC to be at all successful, demands especially maturity of judgment and experience.
1 CONT
1 CONT It may further be said that the well-known words of Horace (Satires, ii. 1, 33), in which he characterizes the vivid portraiture of his life, character and thoughts, which Lucilius bequeathed to the world, "quo fit ut omnis Votiva
1 CONC  pateat veluti descripta tabella Vita senis," lose much of their force unless senis is to be taken in its ordinary sense--which it cannot be if Lucilius died at the age of forty-six.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucilius spent the greater part of his life at Rome, and died, according to Jerome, at Naples. He belonged to the equestrian order, a fact indicated by Horace's notice of himself as infra Lucili censum. Though not himself belongin
1 CONC g to any of the great senatorial families, he was in a position to associate with them on equal terms. This circumstance contributed to the boldness, originality and thoroughly national character of his literary work. Had he been 
1 CONC a semi-Graecus, like Ennius and Pacuvius, or of humble origin, like Plautus, Terence or Accius, he would scarcely have ventured, at a time when the senatorial power was strongly in the ascendant, to revive the rôle which had prove
1 CONC d disastrous to Naevius; nor would he have had the intimate knowledge of the political and social life of his day which fitted him to be its painter. Another circumstance determining the bent of his mind was the character of the t
1 CONC ime. The origin of Roman political and social satire is to be traced to the same disturbing and disorganizing forces which led to the revolutionary projects and legislation of the Gracchi.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reputation which Lucilius enjoyed in the best ages of Roman literature is proved by the terms in which Cicero and Horace speak of him. Persius, Juvenal and Quintilian vouch for the admiration with which he was regarded in the 
1 CONC first century of the empire. The popularity which he enjoyed in his own time is attested by the fact that at his death, although he had filled none of the offices of state, he received the honour of a public funeral. His chief cla
1 CONC im to distinction is his literary originality. He may be called the inventor of poetical satire, as he was the first to impress upon the rude inartistic medley, known to the Romans by the name of satura, that character of aggressi
1 CONC ve and censorious criticism of persons, morals, manners, politics, literature, etc. which the word satire has ever since denoted.
1 CONT
1 CONT In point of form the satire of Lucilius owed nothing to the Greeks. It was a legitimate development of an indigenous dramatic entertainment, popular among the Romans before the first introduction of the forms of Greek art among th
1 CONC em; and it seems largely also to have employed the form of the familiar epistle. But the style, substance and spirit of his writings were apparently as original as the form. He seems to have commenced his poetical career by ridicu
1 CONC ling and parodying the conventional language of epic and tragic poetry, and to have used the language commonly employed in the social intercourse of educated men. Even his frequent use of Greek words, phrases and quotations, repre
1 CONC hended by Horace, was probably taken from the actual practice of men, who found their own speech as yet inadequate to give free expression to the new ideas and impressions which they derived from their first contact with Greek phi
1 CONC losophy, rhetoric and poetry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Further, he not only created a style of his own, but, instead of taking the substance of his writings from Greek poetry, or from a remote past, he treated of the familiar matters of daily life, of the politics, the wars, the admin
1 CONC istration of justice, the eating and drinking, the money-making and money-spending, the scandals and vices, which made up the public and private life of Rome in the last quarter of the 2nd century BC. This he did in a singularly f
1 CONC rank, independent and courageous spirit, with no private ambition to serve, or party cause to advance, but with an honest desire to expose the iniquity or incompetence of the governing body, the sordid aims of the middle class, an
1 CONC d the corruption and venality of the city mob. There was nothing of stoical austerity or of rhetorical indignation in the tone in which he treated the vices and follies of his time.
1 CONT
1 CONT His character and tastes were much more akin to those of Horace than of either Persius or Juvenal. But he was what Horace was not, a thoroughly good hater; and he lived at a time when the utmost freedom of speech and the most unre
1 CONC strained indulgence of public and private animosity were the characteristics of men who took a prominent part in affairs. Although Lucilius took no active part in the public life of his time, he regarded it in the spirit of a man 
1 CONC of the world and of society, as well as a man of letters. His ideal of public virtue and private worth had been formed by intimate association with the greatest and best of the soldiers and statesmen of an oldergeneration.
1 CONT
1 CONT The remains of Lucilius extend to about eleven hundred, mostly unconnected lines, most of them preserved by late grammarians, as illustrative of peculiar verbal usages. He was, for his time, a voluminous as well as a very discursi
1 CONC ve writer. He left behind him thirty books of satires, and there is reason to believe that each book, like the books of Horace and Juvenal, was composed of different pieces. The order in which they were known to the grammarians wa
1 CONC s not that in which they were written. The earliest in order of composition were probably those numbered from xxvi. to xxix., which were written in the trochaic and iambic metres that had been employed by Ennius and Pacuvius in th
1 CONC eir Saturae.
1 CONT
1 CONT In these he made those criticisms on the older tragic and epic poets of which Horace and other ancient writers speak. In them too he speaks of the Numantine War as recently finished, and of Scipio as still living. Book i., on the 
1 CONC other hand, in which the philosopher Carneades, who died in 128, is spoken of as dead, must have been written after the death of Scipio.
1 CONT
1 CONT Most of the satires of Lucilius were written in hexameters, but, so far as an opinion can be formed from a number of unconnected fragments, he seems to have written the trochaic tetrameter with a smoothness, clearness and simplici
1 CONC ty which he never attained in handling the hexameter. The longer fragments produce the impression of great discursiveness and carelessness, but at the same time of considerable force. He appears, in the composition of his various 
1 CONC pieces, to have treated everything that occurred to him in the most desultory fashion, sometimes adopting the form of dialogue, sometimes that of an epistle or an imaginary discourse, and often to have spoken in his own name, givi
1 CONC ng an account of his travels and adventures, or of amusing scenes that he had witnessed, or expressing the results of his private meditation and experiences.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like Horace he largely illustrated his own observations by personal anecdotes and fables. The fragments clearly show how often Horace has imitated him, not only in expression, but in the form of his satires (see for instance i. 5 
1 CONC and ii. 2), in the topic which he treats of, and the class of social vices and the types of character which he satirizes.
0 @N6000000000437011702@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Praetor Of Rome
0 @N6000000000437364882@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Praetor Of Rome
0 @N6000000002187712744@ NOTE Marcus Atius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Marcus Atius Balbus (105 - 51BC) was the son and heir of an elder Balbus (148 - 87 BC) and Pompeia Strabo. Strabo was sister to Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo, father of Pompey the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT The family of the elder Balbus came from a senatorial family from Aricia (modern Ariccia, Italy). In villas, relatives would display masks in memory of their ancestors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Balbus married Julia Caesaris, sister of Julius Caesar. They had 3 daughters Atia Balba Prima, Atia Balba Caesonia (mother of Caesar Augustus) and Atia Balba Tertia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Balbus served as a praetor and under Caesar was appointed as a commissioner under Julian Law, to divide the estates in Campania among the commoners.
